# Playtest notes / backlog

## Done

Moved to [`doc/dev/history.md`](doc/dev/history.md) — this file is the working backlog now.

Durable rationale ("why is the code like this?") belongs in [`doc/dev/decisions.md`](doc/dev/decisions.md); the chronological record, including approaches measured and reverted, is in `history.md`.

## Open

- [ ] **Elites are far too strong, and they are NOT rare — scale the HP down; the "make them occur" half is already solved by real code (2026-08-05, corrected same day).** Original measurement covered the 8-repo `balancing_corpus/`. Extending it to `wolf3d`/`stb`/`curl`/`laravel`/`ripgrep` (2,535 levels, 63,232 enemies, 28,178 callable entities) **refuted the rarity half outright**, so the numbers below replace an earlier version of this item rather than refining it.
  - **It is procedural style, not language, age or size — measured across 19 repos.** The first corpus reported **4 Elites in 11,615 enemies (0.03%), max 3,600 HP** and concluded a clamp would fix something happening four times in eight repositories. Those 8 were all modern, method-decomposed code. Elites per 1,000 enemies across the full corpus:

    | repo | lang | per 1k | max Elite HP (hard) |
    |---|---|---:|---:|
    | vim | C | **10.9** | **50,400** |
    | stb | C headers | 6.2 | 25,800 |
    | curl | C | 5.5 | 11,850 |
    | wolf3d | C 1992 | 4.3 | 16,725 |
    | doom | C 1993 | 3.8 | 8,925 |
    | **codeenstein3d** | **TypeScript** | **3.1** | 6,150 |
    | git | C | 2.5 | 13,575 |
    | quake | C 1996 | 2.2 | 7,125 |
    | commons-lang / django | Java / Python | 0.7 / 0.5 | ~5,000 |
    | ripgrep / laravel | Rust / PHP | 0.2 / 0.0 | 3,525 / 25,050 |
    | leveldb, sinatra, serilog, flask | C++, Ruby, C#, Python | **0.0** | — |

    Seven of the top eight are C. Everything decomposed into methods sits at or near zero **including the mature and enormous** — django is 935 levels at 0.5, laravel 1,694 levels with a single Elite. So the driver is long procedural functions, not age (leveldb and commons-lang are mature and produce ~none) and not scale.
  - **The ceiling is 50,400 HP, not 3,600.** `vim`'s `nfa_emit_equi_class` (`src/regexp_nfa.c`) is complexity **672**, and vim holds 7 of the 10 largest enemies the generator can produce from real code. For scale: the player's entire rocket reserve is worth ~**2,130 damage**, and `demo-campaign` level 17 — which kills 100% of bot runs reaching it — carries 9,675 Elite HP *spread across three*. One vim function is **24x the whole reserve**.
  - **This is not someone else's problem: your own repo ranks 6th.** `codeenstein3d` generates 12 Elites from its own source at 3.1 per 1k — ahead of git, Quake, Java and Python — topped by `runMultiplayerSessionAsGuest` at 6,150 HP, with `simulate` (engine.ts) and `renderMinimap`/`renderScene` (raycaster.ts) close behind. Six of those come from the bundled `demo-campaign/` fixtures; the engine-code-only figure is ~1.6 per 1k, still above Java, Python and Rust.
  - **A whole class of repos generates nothing at all.** `microsoft/MS-DOS` is 85% Assembly and there is no `.asm` adapter, so it produces almost no levels. Worth deciding whether that is acceptable or whether historic/assembly repos deserve a story.
  - **The threshold is not the problem; the multiplier and the missing ceiling are.** Complexity distributions, old corpus vs these five:

    | | median | p99 | max | c>=40 | HP at c=40 (hard) |
    |---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|
    | original 8 repos | 1 | 19 | 93 | 0.16% | 3,000 |
    | these 5 repos | 1 | **35** | **348** | **0.77%** | 3,000 |

    Elites already occur at ~5x the assumed rate and the tail runs to complexity **348**, so **(b) needs no change at all** — lowering `ELITE_COMPLEXITY_THRESHOLD` would now make a bad problem far worse rather than solving anything. The remaining work is entirely (a): `hp = complexity * 25 * 2 * difficulty` is linear and **uncapped**, and real code supplies the complexity to exploit it. A ceiling, or a fraction of the level's obtainable damage, is the whole fix.
  - **A related mapping quirk this surfaced**: `.h`/`.H` files become *bonus* levels (boosted ammo, trivial — demo's one bonus level is 0% deaths at clear ratio 49.95). That makes **wolf3d 63% bonus levels and stb 69%**, since uppercase `.H` lowercases to `h`. Most of the original Wolfenstein source therefore generates trivial levels. Worth deciding whether a header should really be a bonus level, or whether that rule was meant for something narrower.
  - **Do not lower the threshold without addressing the cliff.** `ELITE_HP_MULTIPLIER` stacks on *un-split* HP, so crossing the threshold always doubles a room's total and concentrates it into one target — c=39 spawns a 4-enemy 976 HP pack, c=40 spawns one 2,000 HP enemy. That discontinuity is already firing 217 times across these five repos, so the ramp idea (soften between ~30 and ~50) was never cosmetic — it is load-bearing today, not conditional on some future threshold change.
  - **History worth not repeating**: `ELITE_HP_MULTIPLIER` was already cut **4 -> 2** on 2026-07-30 after the bot died 12/12 on level 12's Elite (`enemies.ts:26-37`). That fixed level 12 — it is at 0% deaths across 156 runs now — and did nothing for levels 15/17. A second HP cut alone would likely repeat that pattern.
  - **Levers**: `ELITE_COMPLEXITY_THRESHOLD`, `ELITE_HP_MULTIPLIER`, `HP_PER_COMPLEXITY` (all `src/map/generation/enemies.ts:11-38`), composing multiplicatively with `DIFFICULTY_MULTIPLIERS.hard.hp` (1.5, `src/difficulty.ts`).
  - **Acceptance needs splitting — half of this is contradicted (2026-08-06).** A corpus sweep *can* verify the ceiling itself ("no single Elite above N"), and that half stands. It **cannot** verify that a clamp changes lethality: on wolf3d, the one Elite-driven repo captured, solver output correlates with observed death rate at **ρ = −0.02**. Any lethality claim needs a bot capture. See `balance-review-2026-08-04.md` §13.
  - **The "arithmetic impossibility" framing is withdrawn (2026-08-06).** `balancing:budget`'s own gate — every enemy killable with the damage obtainable on its level — **passes on all 23 repos**. vim's worst level (`src/regexp_bt.c`, 6 Elites / 134,025 HP) sits at clear ratio 4.85, and its 50,325 HP Elite is 7.1% of that level's obtainable damage. The case against huge Elites is *time-to-kill under fire*, not unkillability.
  - **And the measurement is contaminated: fix the bot first.** The bot knife-trades Elites — **305 knife swings** into a 3,000 HP Elite, **2 ghidra shots across 25 deaths**, ending each level holding *more* rockets than it started, because closing to 0.54t puts it inside `ROCKET_SAFE_DISTANCE`. Every Elite death rate on record is an upper bound produced by the worst available tactic. The `MELEE_MAX_TARGET_HP:500` A/B removed the knife trade (305→0 swings) and moved lethality not at all (100% → 89%, Fisher p=0.24). See review §14.
  - ~~**Related and deliberately separate**: the corpus's real outlier is level size, not Elite HP…~~ **REFUTED, do not write this item (2026-08-06).** Its headline example, ripgrep's `flags/defs.rs` at 432 enemies / 8,061 DPS, is contradicted by levels of the same size that every run cleared: sinatra's `base.rb` (**439 enemies / 7,184 dps**) and laravel's `Eloquent/Model.php` (**394 / 6,372**) each killed **nobody**, while curl's `chkspeed.c` at **7 enemies / 125 dps** killed **100%**. Size is not the axis. Review §12. **Write an attrition item instead** — regular + Edge Case enemies deal **75–100% of all damage taken** in every repo (Elites at most a quarter), and have no backlog entry. Note Elites still dominate the *final* level (62–98%): they are not the attrition, they are the full stop.

- [x] **Lane parallelism was per-combo, so a finished lane idled for the rest of the run — CHUNK STEALING DONE 2026-08-07; cross-repo pipelining still open.** `runLane` (`scripts/lib/laneOrchestrator.mjs`) pulled whole *combos* off a shared queue and `driveCombo` owned one end to end. With N combos and N lanes there was nothing left to steal the moment a lane finished, so it stopped working while the run continued.
  - **Fixed by making a lane claim an *invocation*, not a combo.** A free lane now takes the next chunk of whichever combo is furthest from its target and has room for another. Four things had to change together, and each was a real hazard rather than a tidy-up: sequence numbers come from a per-combo counter instead of `fileCount + 1` (two concurrent invocations computed the *same* number and would have overwritten each other's output file, log and event-log directory — silent loss of a whole chunk, since the output file only appears at the very end of a ~50-minute invocation); `spawned` moved from per-lane to per-combo, or `MAX_INVOCATIONS` would have become a per-lane cap and the cost bound would have multiplied by lane count; `envFor` receives `inFlightBefore` so the capture reserves the shortfall instead of re-reading it off disk, without which every extra lane re-runs the same remaining work; and each `Runner` still runs exactly one invocation at a time, which is what keeps `sshRunner`'s single `REMOTE_DIR` per host and the multiplayer campaign's fixed ports 8788/5174 safe.
  - **Measured on the real orchestrator with real child processes**, 2 combos / 3 lanes: one-lane-per-combo leaves **lane 3 with 0 invocations and 0ms busy — idle for the entire run**, 34% idle lane-time; with stealing all three lanes work, idle falls to **12%** and wall time drops 700ms → 524ms. The control arm is the point: it reproduces the reported failure, so the improvement is not just a number that looks better.
  - **Deliberately NOT enabled for `run-balancing-campaign.mjs` or the multiplayer campaign** — both default to `maxConcurrentPerCombo: 1`, i.e. exactly the old behaviour. They size chunks from a fixed `BATCH_SIZE` rather than from remaining work, so stealing there would overshoot by whole batches, and the multiplayer one cannot run two local invocations at all (fixed ports, `--strictPort`). Enabling it for them needs the shortfall-reservation change first.
  - **Measured, not theoretical.** The ripgrep capture ran 22:34-00:46 (132 min) on three lanes. Casual's last event write was 22:51 — that lane was **idle for 115 of 132 minutes**. Across all three lanes roughly half the available lane-time produced nothing, and this is the single largest inefficiency in the whole capture pipeline. A five-repo sweep pays it five times.
  - **Still open — cross-repo pipelining, between runs.** Today captures are strictly sequential because staging is a working-tree operation (`demo-campaign/` swapped per repo) and the local lane reads that tree, so no branch may change mid-capture. **Remote lanes do not have this problem** — each has its own checkout at `REMOTE_DIR` and `bootstrapHost` force-checks-out a sha. If the sha were per-combo rather than per-run, a remote lane could start the *next repo* while the local lane finishes the current one. That is the bigger win and the bigger change.
  - **Acceptance — met.** Idle lane-minutes are now instrumented and printed in the capture summary (`Lane utilisation over ...` / `idle lane-time: ...`). The number was invisible before, and that is exactly how a lane sat idle for 115 of 132 minutes without anyone noticing: the only symptom is a quiet machine.
  - Note the earlier "cheap mitigation" — capture `normal` alongside `hard` for 6 combos on 3 lanes — is no longer needed *for this reason*. It is still worth doing for the data, but it roughly doubles capture time and does not come free, contrary to what the remediation order originally assumed.

- [ ] **The browser and the route planner can disagree about which file is level 1, and nothing catches it — 58 runs were lost to this (2026-08-06).** `planLevels` (`run-balancing-telemetry.mjs`) plans in **filename order** and hands the bot a route for slot 1. The game picks its entry point with `findEntrypointByScanning` (`main.ts:2250`), which takes the **lowest-complexity file that has `complexity > 0`**. A staged campaign whose slot 1 parses to complexity 0 therefore desynchronises the two, silently.
  - **Measured on curl.** Planner slot 1 = `01_projects_OS400_make-docs.sh`. The engine actually loaded `03_docs_examples_pop3-stat.c` — a **zero-diff exact match** against planned index 2, and **205 differing tiles** against index 0.
  - **The rule is `main`, not complexity — an earlier version of this item said otherwise and was wrong.** `findEntrypointByScanning` returns `bestWithMain ?? bestOverall ?? firstParsed` (`main.ts:2243-2257, 2268`): the cheapest file *containing a `main`/`Main` function*, falling back to cheapest overall only if none has one. Measured: `make-docs.sh` scores complexity **1** (not 0 — tree-sitter-bash emits top-level `variable_assignment` nodes that `GLOBAL_CONTAINER` matches) with no `main`; `pop3-stat.c` scores **4** and has `int main(void)`. The more complex file won because it has a `main`. Excluding `.sh` re-aligned the enumerations by changing which file sorted first, not by removing zero-complexity files.
  - **So `stage-campaign.mjs` cannot prevent this at staging time as written.** Its `order()` guard keeps slot 1 at the global complexity minimum, which models only the `bestOverall` branch. Any campaign whose cheapest file lacks `main` while another has one still diverges. Staging-time prevention would have to model `hasMain` (and `partitionEntrypointCandidates`, `main.ts:2135`, which tries non-test files first) — which is an argument for the runtime grid check, which does not care about the rule at all.
  - **What it looks like when it happens**, and why it was misread as a navigation bug for most of a day: the bot walks a route that is valid for a map it is not standing on, and jams against a wall the planner thinks is floor. On curl it pinned at `y=31.75` — exactly the tile boundary at 32.0 minus the 0.2 player radius — while `x` slid west, which is axis-separated collision sliding (`collidesWithWall`, `player.ts:107`), not heading oscillation. The anomaly scan labels it `oscillation` because that is what the position trace looks like from outside.
  - **The pre-flight is not sufficient and should be replaced.** The entrypoint check compares the browser's level-1 **enemy count** against planned slot 1. Both curl candidates had exactly 2 enemies, so it passed. **Only grid equality can detect this**: diff `__codeensteinTestHooks.getGrid()` against `levelPlans[0].map.grid` and refuse to capture on any mismatch. It is one cheap comparison and it would have saved 58 runs. `scripts/diagnose-level-wedge.mjs` already loads both sides, so the check has a natural home.
  - **The real fix is to stop having two enumerations.** Either `planLevels` adopts the browser's entrypoint rule, or the browser plays in the planner's order. Today there are three divergent notions of "level 1" (game, solver, planner) and this is the second time that has cost real runs — the first was the wolf3d entrypoint inversion fixed in `4a27b91`.
  - **Note the interaction with that fix.** `complexity > 0` is *from* `4a27b91`; before it the scan picked the most complex file, which was worse. The condition is not wrong — the planner not knowing about it is.
  - **Beware when staging**: `stage-campaign.mjs` filters zero-complexity levels using the *solver's* complexity, which is not the same number the browser's parser computes. curl's `make-docs.sh` passed that filter and still scored 0 in the browser.

- [x] **A C# file generated 640 doors and 321 keys and could not be routed — FIXED (2026-08-06).** `src/parser/generic/refinements.ts` defaulted an unmodified C# method to `private`. That is right inside a class and wrong everywhere else, and the "everywhere else" turned out to include a case nobody expected.
  - **The first diagnosis was incomplete.** It looked like "interface members are implicitly public and the parser says private". True, but not why it happened here: `tree-sitter-c-sharp` **fails outright** on Serilog's `ILogger.cs` — its `#if FEATURE_DEFAULT_INTERFACE` directives yield an ERROR tree, and **134 of its 136 methods have `ERROR` as their immediate parent**, with no `interface_declaration` anywhere above them. An interface-aware default would not have fired at all; only measuring the real ancestor chain showed that.
  - **The rule is now "private requires evidence."** `inCSharpPrivateByDefaultBody` returns true only when it can positively see an enclosing `class`/`struct`/`record`; an interface, or a body it cannot identify, yields `public`. Explicit modifiers still win, so C# 8+ interface members with an access modifier are unaffected.
  - **Why it mattered so much.** `isLockableRoom` locks private/protected *method* rooms and `placeDoors` turns every corridor mouth of a locked room into a door. 136 wrongly-private methods became 140 lockable rooms: **640 doors, 321 keys**, ~4.6 doors per room against 0.19 on the class-bearing file beside it. `planRoute` opens one door per iteration and gives up at `MAX_ITERATIONS = 200`, returning `{ok:false, legs:286}` — and legs are populated even on failure, which is why the diagnostic reported "286 legs" while `driveLegs` never ran and the attempt showed **0 decisions, 0 tiles**.
  - **Measured after the fix**: `ILogger.cs` parses **136 public** methods, the level drops to **0 doors and 0 keys**, and `planRoute` returns `ok:true` with **1 leg**. Across all 105 staged levels in the seven captured repos, levels that fail to plan went **1 → 0**. Full suite 4,785 passing.
  - **Still open, and worth its own look**: the grammar failure itself. `ILogger.cs` "parses" — it returns 140 entities — while its tree is largely an ERROR node, so complexity, nesting and visibility from any C# file with preprocessor directives are unreliable rather than absent. Nothing surfaces that today; a parse-error signal on `ParsedFile` would let both the generator and `balancing:budget` say so instead of silently guessing.

- [ ] **INVESTIGATE, do not "fix": is `expectedDamagePerShot` calibrated? The ghidra item's premise was wrong (rewritten 2026-08-06).** The earlier version of this item said `pickRangedWeapon`'s `clusterCount >= 2` branch short-circuits to a shotgun before the HP-aware scoring loop ever runs, and that removing the short-circuit would let the bot rocket an Elite. **The first half is true and the second is not.**
  - **The scorer independently prefers the shotgun.** Running the real module against a 3,000 HP Elite at 5.1 tiles (`rockets: 19`), lower is better: Casual shotgun **22.2** vs ghidra **88.7**; Gamer **17.0** vs **46.1**; Pro **16.2** vs **35.1**. Ghidra only wins past **~8.5 tiles** (Pro ~8.2, Gamer ~8.6). So the branch and the economics agree, and deleting the short-circuit changes nothing at that range.
  - **Why: the shotgun's hit model saturates.** `expectedDamagePerShot` gives an Elite a 1.5x sprite scale, so `halfWidthPx` (41.2) exceeds `scatterPx` (36.8) at 5.1 tiles and `hitFraction` clamps to **1.0** — the model credits the shotgun its full **175 damage per blast** out to ~5.6 tiles. 3000/175 = 18 blasts x 0.85s = 15.3s, against ghidra's 20 rockets x 1.1s = 22.0s before any penalty.
  - **The open question, and the actual work.** The 2026-08-06 capture measured the shotgun delivering **79.6 damage per shot** into that Elite (6,050 over 76 shots) and ghidra **93** (1,951 over 21) — against 175 and 150 predicted. If the model is ~2.2x optimistic on the shotgun, weapon choice is wrong *everywhere*, not just in one branch, and fixing `expectedDamagePerShot` would correct it without overriding anything. If instead the gap is aim error — real fights move, the model assumes a static target — the model is fine and the bot's accuracy is the story. **Nobody knows which, and that is the task**: compare predicted vs measured damage-per-shot per weapon per range bucket across the captures, using `shot`/`damageDealt` events which already carry `w` and `dist`.
  - **Two blockers for anyone tempted to shortcut it.** Casual omits ghidra from `weaponPriority` **entirely** — deliberate, and `profiles.mjs:131-135` argues for it at length ("membership, not order, is what this list controls") — so a cluster-branch override would hand Casual a weapon its profile deliberately excludes. And any `weaponPriority` edit changes `profilesHash()`, which `profiles.test.mjs` pins against `defaultHighscore.ts`; the suite then fails until a regeneration that `remediation-order-2026-08-06.md` says must come **last**.
  - **What is genuinely wrong regardless**, and cheap: the cluster branch's shotgun fallback (`combatPolicy.mjs:1245`) is unconditional on the target and never consults `targetHp`, so it is the same "asks how far, never how big" shape as the melee bug. Worth fixing for coherence even though it changes no outcome at 5 tiles. There is **no test asserting ghidra is ever selected** — every ghidra assertion in `combatPolicy.test.mjs` is negative — so the fallback is unpinned in both directions.

- [ ] **Flamethrower (Friday Hotfix): give it real reach and replace the hard cutoff with distance falloff (2026-08-05).** Today `maxRange: 3.5` (`weapons.ts:272`) is a **binary gate**, not a range curve: in `resolveShot` a pellet whose target is beyond it becomes `{kind: "miss"}` outright (`engine.ts:4495-4499`), so the weapon deals full 8-per-pellet damage at 3.4 tiles and literally nothing at 3.6. A flame jet should thin out, not stop at a wall.
  - **The precedent is already in the codebase.** `rocketDamageAt` (`rockets.ts:116-121`) is exactly the shape wanted: `max(FLOOR, 1 - distance / radius)` scaling the damage, zero past the radius. The flamethrower wants the same treatment with a plateau — full damage out to roughly today's 3.5, then decaying to zero somewhere around 6-7 tiles.
  - **Why the hard cap exists, and why falloff serves that goal better.** `weapons.ts:252-259` records that cone spread alone let it connect from further out "than a real flamethrower should ever reach", so the cap was added as the thing that actually enforces reach. Falloff enforces the same intent more honestly: reach is bounded by damage becoming negligible rather than by an invisible wall, and the tight-jet-fanning-at-the-tip visual (`FlameStream`) already implies decay the mechanics don't deliver.
  - **The data says it is locked to the wrong band.** Over the 2026-08-04 capture: **11,874 trigger-pulls, 64.6% hit rate, 5.4% kill share**. Hit rate by engagement range runs 76% (0-2t) / 66% (2-4t) / 33% (4-6t) — but that last bucket rests on **30 pellets out of ~71,000**, because 3.5 tiles cuts it off before it gets there. Meanwhile fights against *regular* enemies happen at a median of **4.6 tiles**, outside its reach entirely, while Edge Cases sit at 2.7t. The weapon is confined to the band that holds the 10-15 HP trash.
  - **It is already the highest-DPS weapon in the game, so the falloff is what pays for the range.** 48 damage per trigger-pull at `fireIntervalSec: 0.1` is ~480 DPS, and `ammoPerShot: 2.5` exists specifically because a full gas pool would otherwise outdamage everything else. Extending reach *without* decay would be a straight buff to the strongest damage profile in the table — the two changes are a tuned pair, like the shotgun's cadence/damage pair.
  - **Touchpoints, including the one that fails silently.** `Weapon` needs the falloff fields (`weapons.ts:64`), `resolveShot` needs to attach a damage scale to the pellet outcome instead of discarding it, and the damage call site must apply it. Then the **bot's mirrored table**, which `adding-a-weapon.md` flags as the silent-failure step — 3.5 is duplicated in *two* places there: `FRIDAY_HOTFIX_MAX_RANGE` (`combatPolicy.mjs:318`) and `WEAPON_STATS[5].maxRange` (`:910`). Leave those and the bot keeps scoring the weapon as if it stopped at 3.5, which would quietly invalidate any A/B of the change.
  - **Known cost: this invalidates every replay.** `WEAPONS` is folded into `SIMULATION_BALANCE` wholesale (`balanceHash.ts`), so any damage-curve change alters the hash and `defaultHighscore.ts` must be regenerated — a ~35-minute bot run. Per the acid-decay entry below: **regenerate last**, after every other simulation change has landed, not in the middle.
  - **What would show it worked**: pellets actually landing in the 4-6t bucket (today 30 of ~71k) and a kill share moving off 5.4%, *without* total gas-pool damage per level rising — the falloff should redistribute its damage across range, not add to it. Re-runnable from the same seed set as the 2026-08-04 capture for a like-for-like comparison.

- [ ] **Regular and Edge Case enemies do 75-100% of the damage and have no backlog item (2026-08-06).** Written in place of the refuted "level size is the real outlier" bullet. Across the 7-repo sweep, pooling raw `damageTaken.by`: Elites are **at most a quarter** of all damage a run takes (wolf3d 24.8%, codeenstein3d 21.3%, laravel 10.4%, curl 10.0%, ripgrep and the two zero-Elite repos 0%), while regular + Edge Case do the rest. Elites dominate only the *final* level (62-98%) — they are the full stop, not the attrition.
  - **ripgrep is the pure case**: 0% Elite damage, its single Elite never reached, and it still lost every one of 45 runs. An Elite clamp would do nothing for it.
  - **Deliberately not a proposed fix.** This is a hypothesis about where to look, not a measured problem with a known size — and per `remediation-order-2026-08-06.md` it should not be sized until the bot fixes land, since the current numbers are contaminated by the bot walking 14,000 tiles for loot and knife-trading Elites.
  - **What would size it**: after Stage C's re-measure, the share of deaths where no Elite was present at all, and the damage-per-enemy the attrition archetypes actually deliver. Review §10.

- [ ] **`fetch-online-wads.mjs` failing takes `npm run dev` and `npm run build` down with it — it should degrade, not fail (2026-08-04).** The script `process.exit(1)`s on any download or parse failure (`scripts/fetch-online-wads.mjs:95-98`) and runs as the `predev`/`prebuild` hook, so a clone with no network — or one external host being down, or a catalog entry that has gone 404 — can neither start the dev server nor build. Nothing in the online WAD catalog is load-bearing: the game ships procedural default textures, `public/wads/` is gitignored, and a missing pack already degrades cleanly at runtime. It should warn and continue with whatever it managed to fetch, leaving the sidebar's online-texture-pack list short (or empty) instead of making the app unbuildable. The script is already idempotent — it skips any destination that exists (`:48-51`) — so a partial fetch self-heals on the next run with network. Worth deciding whether a strict mode survives somewhere for CI, which *should* notice a dead catalog entry rather than silently shipping a shorter list.

- [x] **A door key could be placed behind an Exception Zone's acid gauntlet — FIXED (2026-08-02).** Found while investigating a level-6 telemetry regression. `placeKeys` treated the `try`/`catch`/`finally` rects as ordinary reachable floor, so a key could land past the gauntlet: **4 of the 7 campaign levels that have a zone**. That inverts the zone's whole design — `decisions.md` justifies the gauntlet being unavoidable acid, spikes and a mine *because entering is a choice*; a key past it makes the toll mandatory, which no other locked door in the game charges. All three rects now join the same exclusion set `placeKeys` already used for corridor-feature tiles. Key counts per level are unchanged — they move, none are lost.
  - **The bot's own trace said so and the aggregate hid it.** 37 of 39 `healthDrainFrozen` events on `stage06_pipeline.py` carried `activity=route`, not `loot`, and 34 carried `branch=hazard` — it was walking its planned route, and `planRoute` genuinely went `openDoor(6,41) -> finally room -> back down -> exit`. Reading the per-level totals first sent me chasing "the gauntlet moved onto the main route", which was the wrong frame.
  - **Pre-existing, not a regression from the layout rework** — baseline `7af288f` had it on **5 of 8** zone-levels. What changed is *which* levels draw it: the scan only covers levels 1-8, baseline's affected levels (11/15/17) were all outside that window, and the reshuffle moved it onto `stage06`. A latent bug that the rework exposed rather than caused.
  - Measured, same 8-level/5-attempt/3-profile scan, `ticks/1k decisions` (the normalisation `balancing-telemetry.md` prescribes): `healthDrainFrozen` **1.3 -> 0.1**, `stall` **47.3 -> 21.1**, total anomaly ticks **5714 -> 4226**. Level 6's gauntlet drain events: baseline 10, pre-fix 39, **fixed 6**.

- [x] **The bot could not get out of acid, and it accounted for most of every "death" number this project had recorded — FIXED 2026-08-07 after three failed attempts.** The fix is one line: cap the hazard branch's turning burst by distance as well as by turn angle. Found in the first hours of the Stage C re-measure. `decide()`'s hazard branch was the highest-priority branch and it did the wrong thing: it marched toward **`navTarget`** — wherever the bot already happened to be headed — rather than toward safety, and it only ran at all `if (map && navTarget && ...)`, so a bot burning between route legs had no hazard handling whatsoever.
  - **Signature, identical across four repositories and two bot versions**: a median **~7s** of continuous acid and **~125 damage ticks** — a full health bar — with **no combat at all** (0 of 20 serilog cases had a single shot fired or any enemy damage during the exposure). The bot enters the hazard at roughly full health and stays until dead. serilog level 10 has **12 acid tiles out of 1,025 walkable**; crossing takes under a second.
  - **Share of deaths that were hazard, not an enemy**: Stage C serilog **62 of 73 (85%)**, and in the *previous* sweep's stored data ripgrep **29 of 32 (90%)**, wolf3d **38-58%** across five variants, codeenstein3d **28 of 59 (47%)**. sinatra, curl and laravel are 0% — the defect only bites where the staged levels contain acid, which is why it hid for so long.
  - **Consequence for everything measured before this date**: in those repositories a large fraction of deaths were not caused by an enemy. Note the asymmetry that made it invisible in the damage tables — on Stage C serilog hazard is only **8.7% of damage taken** but **85% of deaths**, because a hazard death drains a whole health bar rather than contributing a slice. Any lethality claim resting on death counts for ripgrep, wolf3d, codeenstein3d or serilog needs re-reading.
  - **Two fix attempts, both measured, both WORSE THAN THE BUG — reverted in full.** Same staged serilog campaign, Casual, 60 runs per arm, only the bot differing:
    - baseline (march at `navTarget`): **50%** death rate, 30 of 60
    - attempt 1, steer at nearest safe tile (`c1b3d06`): **100%**, 101 of 101
    - attempt 2, + prefer the exit the bot faces + commit to it (`e3ec983`): **100%**, 60 of 60
    Attempt 2 was aimed at a mechanism that is real but was not the cause: the branch spends a *turn*-sized burst when it must rotate past `TURN_MOVE_EPS`, so aiming behind the bot burns decisions rotating. Fixing that changed nothing, which means the diagnosis was wrong, not incomplete.
  - **The fact that reframes it, and that both attempts ignored**: **all 360** pre-fix runs take hazard damage on level 10, and **83% survive it**. Acid contact is routine and survivable; only a subset dies. So the bot does *not* simply lack an escape — something specific happens in the fatal subset. Overriding the branch to steer at safety removed whatever accidental escape the old marching produced and made every run fatal.
  - **MECHANISM FOUND 2026-08-07, from a real trace — it is an overshoot orbit around a waypoint the bot is standing on.** Instrumentation added first (both kept, both behaviour-neutral): a `[hazard]` per-decision line in `decide()`, because the general `[nav]` line is emitted *after* this branch returns and so went silent for exactly the decisions of interest; and a trace/grid dump on the diagnostic's `DIED` path, which previously dumped nothing (only `STUCK` did). Three serilog level-10 death traces then showed, unambiguously:
    - the nav target sits **0.16 tiles away — the bot is standing on it**, and the branch has **no arrival check**, so it keeps steering at it
    - it turns to face it, takes one sprint step, overshoots completely (`delta` flips **-0.43 -> -2.65**), re-aims, repeats
    - ~110 decisions per death, **33-38 tiles walked for 0.4-1.9 tiles of net displacement**, **5.4-5.8 complete rotations**, `|delta|` above threshold in 93-98% of decisions
    It was never stuck, never merely "failing to escape" — it circles a waypoint it cannot turn tightly enough to reach, inside the acid.
  - **THE CAUSE — arithmetic, not logic.** The branch holds `KeyW` while sizing its burst with `turnBurstMs`, so the step is scaled to the **turn** and not to the distance: ~0.32 tiles of sprint per decision against an `ARRIVE_EPS` of **0.15**. The bot cannot physically land inside its own arrival radius, so it steps over the waypoint every time and circles it forever. `moveBurstMs` already sizes a burst to travel exactly `dist` — which is why the *aligned* path converges and the turning path never can. **Fix: `turnBurst = Math.min(turnBurstMs(...), moveBurstMs(dist, true, ...))`.** No aim point changes, no key set changes, so the marching that already carried 83% of runs across a hazard is untouched.
  - **Result, same campaign and combos, n=120, only the bot differing**: completion **51% -> 96%**, hazard deaths **58 -> 0**. The five remaining deaths are all enemy-caused (3 ranged, 2 melee, on levels 8/9/14) — deaths that mean what a death should mean.
  - **THREE earlier attempts, all measured at n>=60, all 100% death rate against the 50% baseline, all reverted**: steer at the nearest safe tile; + prefer the face-aligned exit and commit to it; + turn in place before walking, overriding the aim only within 0.75 tiles. Every one of them changed *where* the bot was going, when the defect was *how far it moved per decision*. The third looked narrowly gated and was not — `dist < 0.75` fires in **94%** of hazard decisions, so it was the first attempt in disguise, which is why all three produced the same number.
  - **The measurement that cracked it**: across 1,035 hazard decisions from eight death traces, `dist < ARRIVE_EPS` was true **0 times**. A condition that never fires once in a thousand decisions is worth more than any amount of reasoning about intent.
  - **Warning for the next attempt: do not trust `diagnose-level-wedge` at n=8.** It reported 3/8, 4/8 and 6/8 deaths for baseline and two attempts — noise that looked like signal. The 60-run capture showed 50%, 100%, 100%. Use the capture harness for any verdict here.
  - Related: this is the same *class* as the loot treadmill below — a bot defect wearing the costume of a balance result.

- [ ] **Lanes differ ~2x in speed and nothing measures it — size the work to the lane.** Chunk stealing (2026-08-07) stopped lanes idling *mid*-run, but not the tail: the last chunks are handed out blind, so a slow host can hold one while every other lane sits finished. Measured on the Stage C serilog capture — local 8 invocations, the laptop 7, mcdope.org 4, for the same 20-attempt chunks; that is a ~2x spread already being papered over by stealing. A fourth host (`tbaumer@46...`) and `nas.lan` are now in the pool too, so the spread will only widen.
  - **Measure**: attempts banked / elapsed, per invocation, per lane label. `laneStats` already carries `busyMs` and `invocations`; what is missing is how many attempts each invocation actually produced, which is a `scanExisting` diff around the call (or a read of the invocation's own output file). Persist an EWMA in a gitignored `lane-speed.json` keyed by label, so a run starts with what the last run learned instead of relearning it every time. Seed an unknown host from one calibration chunk rather than assuming it is fast.
  - **Rule: the slowest lane gets the lowest-effort work.** Work units are not interchangeable, so this is a matching problem, not just a sizing one. Rank the pending units by expected cost and pair them against lanes ranked by measured rate — cheapest unit to the slowest lane, most expensive to the fastest. Three levers, in rough order of payoff:
    1. *Cost-ranked assignment* — a unit's cost is `attempts x per-attempt cost`, and per-attempt cost varies a lot by combo: a Casual/hard run that dies on level 2 is cheap, a Pro/normal run that completes all 15 levels is not. The orchestrator currently picks the combo furthest from its target and ignores cost entirely.
    2. *Per-lane chunk sizing* — ask each lane for `targetChunkMinutes * itsRate` attempts, so an invocation costs roughly the same wall time whichever host takes it.
    3. *Tail scheduling* — once remaining work is under one chunk per lane, hand the final chunks to the **fastest** lanes and give the slow ones nothing at all. A slow lane holding the last chunk is the single worst case, because every other lane is finished and idle while it runs.
  - **Trap that will bite whoever implements this**: `run-balancing-capture.mjs`'s `envFor` reserves the shortfall as `inFlightBefore * CHUNK`, which assumes every in-flight invocation was asked for the *same* CHUNK. Variable per-lane chunk sizes break that arithmetic and the combo starts overshooting its target again. The reservation has to track the attempts actually claimed per in-flight invocation, not multiply by a constant.
  - **Second trap**: attempts/min is not a clean speed signal, because it is combo-dependent — a Casual/hard run that dies on level 2 is fast for reasons that have nothing to do with the host. Either normalise per combo or accept that the EWMA only compares like with like across a long run.
  - **First concrete use — decide `nas.lan`'s place in the pool.** It is the user's homelab NAS on old CPUs, and the suspicion is that it is slow enough to be a net drag rather than a lane. That is a measurement, not a guess: if its rate is a small fraction of the others it should either be dropped from `ssh-hosts.env` or, better, kept and fed only the cheapest units under the rule above. A slow lane is only harmful when it holds *expensive* work or the tail — a NAS quietly grinding through cheap Casual/hard chunks costs nothing and still adds throughput.
  - **Acceptance**: per-lane attempts/min printed in the capture summary next to the existing idle-time line, and no lane idle for more than one chunk-duration while any combo is short of target.

- [ ] **Stage C is NOT ready to re-run: a 4-repo canary (2026-08-07) shows only 2 of 6 tested repos would produce usable data.** Cost 20 minutes and saved a ~20-hour sweep. Casual/hard, 20 attempts each, on the fixed bot:
  - **wolf3d 0/20 completed, 16 hazard deaths. codeenstein3d 0/20, 14 hazard deaths.** Same signature as serilog *before* its fix — median exposure 7.6s and 11.0s, both starting from **full health**. So the distance-capped-burst fix (`673e3e7`) is necessary but **not sufficient**: it took serilog from 51% to 96% completion and zero hazard deaths, and did nothing for these two. Whatever differs about their acid encounters is the next thing to find, and the `[hazard]` trace line plus the diagnostic's DIED dump are already in place to find it.
  - **ripgrep and curl bank ZERO runs** — every invocation dies on the Stage A gate: *"the engine is not playing the level the bot is routing on level 1"*, 8 invocations each, then the cap. The gate is behaving correctly; `stage-campaign.mjs` still cannot guarantee slot 1 is the file `findEntrypointByScanning` will pick, because that rule is `bestWithMain` (cheapest file *containing a main*) and the staging orders on complexity alone. Until this is fixed those two repos cannot be captured at all.
  - Working today: **serilog 96%** (post-fix, n=120) and **sinatra 91%** (no acid in its staged levels). laravel untested.
  - **Method note worth keeping**: run a short canary before any long sweep. Two of these four failures are invisible on serilog, which is the repo every fix so far was validated against.

- [ ] **Bot stalls and oscillates — RE-SCOPED 2026-08-06: this is the single biggest blocker in the project, not a navigation nicety.** Filed below as a tuning issue measured in ticks/1k decisions. The multi-repo sweep shows it costs **121 of 405 runs (30%) — more than every balance problem combined**, and it is the reason two repositories score 0% completion:
  - **serilog: 60 of 60 runs wedged, with ZERO deaths on any of 15 slots.** Every `levelEnd` it emits is `cleared` (748 of them); slot 14 emits none at all. Slot 12 logs **743 `stall` anomalies**; slot-13 stalls read `activity=loot threatDist~1.25 hpFrac 1.00->1.00` — stuck mid-loot beside an enemy that is neither killing it nor being killed. ripgrep lost 13/45 and curl 29/60 the same way.
  - **ROOT CAUSE FOUND 2026-08-06: it is a LOOT TREADMILL, not circling.** Reproduced on serilog with `node scripts/diagnose-level-wedge.mjs --god-mode`, wedging on level 9 (72x72): **88,791 decisions, 14,268 tiles walked, of which loot detours are 87%** (`loot+replan` 9,200t / 64%, `loot` 3,291t / 23%) against **12% on the actual route**. The bot is not oscillating in place — it is walking enormous distances, forever, collecting.
    - `maybeDetourForLoot` (`bot.mjs:995`) is called **once per leg** by `driveLegs` and re-picks from scratch every time: BFS to every candidate within `MAX_LOOT_DETOUR_TILES: 20`, nearest path wins. **No hysteresis and no per-level cap** — it will walk 20 tiles for one pickup, then reconsider.
    - **Kills create more drops**, so on a 323-enemy level the pool refills faster than it drains and the loop has no termination condition. This is why big levels wedge and small ones do not.
    - **A second defect feeds it.** `pathToWaypoints` (`pathfind.mjs:81`) walks to the drop's **tile centre** (`p.x + 0.5`), but the engine collects within `AMMO_PICKUP_RADIUS = 0.5` of the **drop itself**. Centre-to-corner is **0.707**, so **21.5% of positions inside a tile are out of reach from its centre**. And only *static* pickups are marked visited — `if (staticUncollected.includes(best)) this.visitedPickups.add(...)` — so a dynamic drop lying near a tile corner is **permanently uncollectable and permanently re-selected**. That is what the `stall` and `heldKeyNoMovement` anomalies at `activity=loot` actually are.
    - **Candidate fixes**, cheapest first: commit to a detour target until collected or explicitly abandoned; cap loot detour distance as a fraction of remaining route distance; walk to the drop coordinate rather than its tile centre; abandon a dynamic drop after N failed approaches. The last two are near-trivial and address the permanent-re-selection half on their own.
  - **A SECOND, INDEPENDENT ROOT CAUSE — FIXED AND VERIFIED 2026-08-07: the bot was rewriting the plan it routes against.** `Bot#startLevel` took `levelPlans[i].map` **by reference**, while `#refreshGridIfChanged` assigns the engine's live grid into `this.map.grid` as doors open. `levelPlans` is built once and reused for every attempt, so attempt 1 permanently rewrote the plan and **every attempt after the first routed against the previous attempt's end-of-level grid**, with every door it had opened already open. This has affected every capture ever run, and **no single-attempt test can see it** — which is why it survived so long. Measured on the demo campaign, 16 levels x 2 attempts, god mode, both arms over the identical level set: sharing the plan gives **6 plan/engine mismatches, every one on attempt 2** (levels 3, 6, 7, 10, 11, 15; up to 27 tiles); copying the grid gives **0**. Fixed in `47d7a54`. Found only because the plan/engine alignment gate had been added days earlier — and the control arm is what makes the "0" mean anything, since a silent check reports 0 too.
  - **Level 17 is NOT a problem — measured 2026-08-07, and the alarming reading was a god-mode artifact.** `stage17_the_monolith.php` (123x123, **36 route legs**, by far the densest level) ran **>30 minutes in god mode without clearing**, blowing past the engine's 43,200-frame replay cap, which looked like an unbounded grind. It is not. God mode removes the death that terminates such an attempt; with a real loadout the bot simply dies. Measured over 18 full-campaign attempts (3 profiles x 6, hard, current code): level 17 is reached by **7 of 18 runs, all 7 die there**, median **89s** of in-game time and max 107s — **11%** of a run that gets that far. It is not even close to the most expensive level: levels 6, 10 and 11 dominate at 12.4%, 10.9% and 10.6% of all campaign time, against level 17's small share purely because so few runs reach it. A full campaign is a median 789s in-game (max 981s), and a 6-attempt chunk at concurrency 6 costs 4m33s-6m44s of wall clock against a **130-minute** watchdog — two orders of magnitude of headroom. **No capture-sizing consequence.** General lesson worth keeping: a god-mode diagnostic measures the bot's navigation, never its cost, because the thing that bounds a real attempt is death.
  - **NOT curl.** curl's 58/58 level-1 wedge was investigated on 2026-08-06 and is a *different bug* — the browser and the route planner disagreed about which file is level 1, so the bot drove a route for one map around another and ground into a wall. Do not count it toward this item; it has its own entry below.
  - **Acceptance**: serilog completes at a rate comparable to sinatra's 88%, and curl runs without excluding `.sh`. Cheapest completion-rate win available — nothing about the balance needs to change for it.
  - Original 2026-08-02 investigation follows; its evidence still stands, only its priority was wrong. The one metric the layout rework made worse, and it survives normalisation: oscillation **4.4 -> 6.6 ticks/1k decisions**. Localised, so the next attempt starts with evidence rather than a hunch:
  - **Not the corridor vocabulary.** Feature-sited oscillations went *down* (5 -> 4). 13 of 22 are inside rooms, which the rework never touched — same count, same sizes, total room floor identical (4653 -> 4658 tiles). What did change is that rooms went from 65% to 81% of walkable space, because corridor floor halved.
  - **15 of 22 are circling, not ping-pong**: `signFlips` ~0-3 of ~70 ticks, ~15 tiles travelled for ~1.8 tiles of net displacement, repeating at *identical coordinates* across every attempt. The remaining ~7 are true ping-pong (`signFlips` ~90%).
  - Two confirmed sites: an open stretch of an 18x18 labyrinth room with no enemies or props anywhere near (`stage02_bootstrap.sh` ~(37,31)), and a one-tile corridor with an **armed spike trap in the adjacent tile** (`stage07_service.rb` ~(21,33)) — where `detectOscillation`'s own `allWaitingOnSpike` exclusion evidently isn't catching it. Worth checking that exclusion before touching navigation at all.
  - **Deliberately not fixed here.** Both look like bot navigation tuning (turn rate vs walk speed vs arrival epsilon), and `balancing-telemetry.md` requires the 3-combo A/B protocol at n=12 for a bot-behaviour change — an n=1 reading cannot carry a directional claim, which that doc records having learned the hard way. Related: the "decouple bot decisions from engine ... should also help with aim oszilattion" backlog item below.

- [x] **Correction to the correction: `verify:multiplayer-transition` was a real bug all along — FIXED (2026-08-03).** The 2026-08-02 entry below concluded the failure was the documented intermittent flake and told the next reader to stop digging. That was wrong, and the disproof was already in the log: **the stall coordinates repeat across runs with different gameplay seeds**, because demo-campaign level 1's layout is deterministic. By 2026-08-02 it was failing four master runs in a row. Three separate defects, see `history.md`:
  - `driveLegs` dropped `reason` on a stuck route, so the script's route-stuck branch never fired and **every** route failure was misreported as a final-approach failure. That mislabel is what made a real bug look like bad luck for two days.
  - The final approach was a bare `driveToward` — a straight line, with no way to clear the exit-room enemy `checkExit()` silently gates on. Now `driveToExit`, hunting that enemy with combat scoped to the hunt.
  - `MultiplayerBot`'s loot detour had drifted from the single-player one it was forked from (no avoid-set, no re-plan, no stuck bail, no `activity` label) — which is why 40% of the distance walked was billed to `route`.
  - Three *further* bugs that only real CI could surface, after the local harness went 6/6 and the job then failed 4 of 5 real runs: a completed level transition read as a teleport and reported as a failure (the host had won); a blocker hunt that passed all six rounds without executing a single decision because the blocker was already on the bot's own tile; and a wedged route treated as terminal when `driveToExit` can re-path from where the legs died.
  - **Reproduced without multiplayer at all**, which the dev box cannot run: `diagnose-level-wedge.mjs --mp-shape` gives a plain `Bot` `MultiplayerBot`'s decision shape on the same map. Good enough to find the exit-gate bug, blind to the other three — it runs on the virtual clock, so no real transport delay and no transition. **For a path only CI can run, the sample size is CI runs.** 4 consecutive master failures -> 1/5 -> 4/5 -> 6/6 -> 10/10, then blocking again.
  - **The old entry's `EXIT_CLEARANCE_TILES` rationale was also wrong.** Enemies are not solid to the player at all — `collidesWithWall` tests map tiles only, and no entity-vs-entity collision exists. The real gate is `exitRoomHasAliveEnemy()`, a `home`-rect test that proximity does not affect, so the clearance cannot have changed it either way. Kept as presentation; comments corrected.

- [x] **~~Correction: `verify:multiplayer-transition` on the layout branch was NOT a regression (2026-08-02).~~ Superseded — see above.** It failed with `host got stuck on the final approach to the exit` and I attributed it to the layout rework, shipped `EXIT_CLEARANCE_TILES` as "the fix", and it failed again identically. I then matched it against the signature `.github/workflows/verify.yml` documents as intermittent and concluded it was luck. **Both conclusions were wrong**, and the second was worse than the first: it closed the investigation. Lesson kept deliberately — *"this matches a known-flaky signature"* is a hypothesis, not a finding, and the cheap test (do the failures repeat at identical coordinates?) takes one grep.

- [x] **Exception Zone acid burns out under you (2026-08-02).** Idea from watching the bot play `stage06_pipeline.py`: the `try` gauntlet should decay like a Mario falling block — touch it, a timer starts, it vanishes — so the forced return trip out of a dead-end zone isn't charged the same toll twice. Shipped as `src/engine/acidDecay.ts`: **acid only** (spikes already cycle, a mine is consumed on trigger), **exception zones only** (`fillHazards` room pools stay permanent terrain), **2.5s** from first contact.
  - **It derives the grid instead of emitting a mutation, and that is the whole design.** A decay *would* satisfy `pendingGridDelta`'s "every mutation is `{value: 0}`" invariant — unlike Acid Overflow's `0 -> HAZARD_TILE` — but the channel is additive-only, so a guest that mispredicts contact could decay a tile the host hasn't and never put it back. Deriving from `touchedAt` every tick retracts as readily as it applies. Same reasoning, and the same safety argument, as `acidOverflow.ts`: the tile set is fixed at generation time and disjoint from every other claimer. `gridVersion` is not bumped either — acid is walkable both ways, so nothing caching on walkability is stale.
  - **`ACID_DECAY_SECONDS` joined `SIMULATION_BALANCE`.** The same recorded inputs now take less damage crossing a gauntlet while every AST byte and the whole enemy roster stay identical — precisely the silent-divergence class `balanceHash` exists for. **This invalidates the board regenerated earlier the same day**, so it needs regenerating *again*. Lesson worth keeping: regenerate `defaultHighscore.ts` **last**, after every simulation change has landed, not in the middle.
  - `renderMinimap` now grid-re-checks `map.hazards` before painting it. That array is the list of tiles acid was *generated* on, not where it currently is, and it was previously painted unconditionally — a burned-out tile would have kept its marker and sent the player the long way round nothing.

- [x] **Map layout rework: rooms pack, corridors vary (2026-08-02).** Complaint was "room placement yields very long corridors, and the corridors barely change / reuse the breakup elements". Rendering every campaign level (`npm run report:level-maps`, new) made the cause obvious and measurable: `connectRooms` chains rooms in *parse order* while `tryPlaceRoom` placed each at an *independent uniform-random position*, so consecutive rooms sat a mean of **59 tiles apart, peaking at 191**, against rooms 4-18 wide — and the breakup pass then had to inject **277 near-identical rooms across 17 levels, 21% of all floor area, all one construction**, to keep the sightlines down. `decisions.md` had already named proximity chaining as the fix and deferred it. Now: `tryGrowRoom` grows each room off its predecessor, `mapSize` is derived from the packed footprint instead of LOC, `connectLoops` adds shortcuts on top of the parse-order spine, corridor width is rolled per leg (1/2/3) with a new staircase shape, and `dressCorridors` replaces the two-element breakup vocabulary with six kinds that never repeat consecutively. Campaign: leg mean **59 -> 22**, max **191 -> 48**, longest straight run **34 -> 11**, corridor features **277 -> 84** over 10+ footprints, floor-within-bbox **22% -> 37%**, loops **5 -> 20**.
  - **Three things were load-bearing and not obvious.** The room *gap* is what feeds the four `sideCandidates` carvers (secret rooms, depots, switchboards, exception zones), which only ever claim untouched rock — packing flush would have deleted all four silently; `verify:campaign` is the gate. A loop shortcut into a private/protected method room *costs a key* (`placeDoors` locks every mouth), so `connectLoops` verifies doorway counts before/after and undoes the carve rather than trying to predict the jittered path — doors/keys per level came out unchanged. And once rooms packed close, the length pass fired **6 times campaign-wide**, which would have deleted the corridor encounter entirely, since `spawnEdgeCaseEnemies` populates those rooms and nothing else — hence the ornament pass.
  - **Balance moved: Edge Case enemies 573 -> 177 campaign-wide.** Stored balancing telemetry predates this and is not comparable.
  - **Scoped anomaly scan, run on both sides (8 levels, 5 attempts, normal, all 3 profiles).** Qualification is unaffected — **3/3 on every profile, before and after** — and total anomalies are **down 22%**: stall 208 -> 157, oscillation 12 -> 6, healthDrainFrozen 62 -> 56. L7 stalls 30 -> 2, L5 20 -> 7, L1 32 -> 14, L8 32 -> 21. **Two levels got worse and one of them is a real finding**, so don't read the aggregate as "no change":
    - **`stage06_pipeline.py` healthDrainFrozen 10 -> 53.** The Exception Handling Zone's `try` gauntlet is a 1-tile-wide shaft of acid/spike/mine/spike/acid behind a door (`tryRect w=1 h=5`). Packing rooms together moved its door onto the main corridor instead of a remote spur, so the bot now routinely walks in chasing the zone's loot and bleeds out. The shaft geometry is `exceptionZones.ts`' own and unchanged. **This generalises the existing rule** recorded in `decisions.md` ("there is no distance at which 'the corridor out of your spawn room is a damage gauntlet' is acceptable" — which is why the spawn room is excluded as an anchor): with rooms packed, *any* anchor can end up beside the main route. **Open question: whether the exclusion should extend beyond the spawn room, or whether this is bot-policy (a human sees acid and decides).** Not a blocker — every profile still qualifies.
    - **`stage03_legacy_api.php` stall 31 -> 56**, and 2 attempts blew the 21600-frame replay cap on it. It is the key-heaviest level (4 doors/4 keys); worth a look if the highscore bot starts failing to qualify there.
  - **`defaultHighscore.ts` is NOT regenerated** — needs a bot run against a dev server; do it before shipping.
  - Two latent bugs fell out of `verify:campaign:playthrough`: its fixture was a single function, and `spawnEnemies` puts an enemy on every function-room's centre right where the corridor out starts, so the walk only ever survived because random placement happened to put spawn and exit 4 tiles apart (now a struct, which spawns nothing); and `walkWaypoints`' `ARRIVE_EPS` (0.15) was *below* half a sprinting tick's travel (~0.16), so any approach landing in that window orbited the waypoint for the full 30s timeout while moving the whole time.

- [x] **Replay storage cut 3.5x by packing frames binary (2026-08-01).** Concern raised about `localStorage` limits. `replayCodec.ts` (`bin1:`) replaces per-frame JSON with a held-key bitfield + flags byte + raw `dt` bits: **1.48 MB of quota -> 0.42 MB**, shipped chunk 424 KB -> 179 KB gzipped. Root cause was *not* "gzip is weak": `dt` carried **14,425 distinct float64 values across 213,740 frames** (rAF jitter in the 12th decimal), each its own ~20-char string, so the dictionary never saw a repeat. Two notes worth keeping: **`localStorage` bills in UTF-16**, so base64 costs 2 bytes of quota per char (the old 756 KB of base64 was really ~1.48 MB); and **no regeneration was needed** — a serialization change, so the board was transcoded from its `gz1:` blob and verified byte-identical, seconds rather than a 35-min bot run. Reads still accept `bin1:`/`gz1:`/bare JSON. Rejected after measuring: float32 `dt` (lossy for 100% of values, and `balanceHash` would NOT catch the resulting drift), XOR-delta `dt` (*larger* after gzip), base64-in-JSON (1.31x worse). Remaining lever: microsecond `dt` quantisation (~2x) — only safe if the live loop quantises too.

- [x] **shotgun needs firing interval, can be fired into semi-auto-mode :D — fixed in the engine, where it should have been all along (2026-08-01).** `Weapon.fireIntervalSec` already existed and `updateFiring`'s semi-auto branch already honoured it; the pistol and shotgun simply defined none, so their fire rate was whatever the input device produced. **Pure data fix, zero engine logic**: shotgun `fireIntervalSec: 0.85` (a pump cycle), pistol `0.15` (~6.6/sec). This closes the loop on the 2026-07-17 *"highscore bot presses fire key so fast, the pistol becomes a smg lmao"* item, which was fixed **bot-side only** (`profile.fireCooldownMs`) and left the engine — and therefore every human player — uncapped. The bot's per-profile rate survives as a *human-hand* limit stacked on top; the two now compose via `max` in both `scoreRangedWeapon` and the fire gate.
  - **The cap alone would have destroyed the shotgun, which is the non-obvious part.** At 0.85s it does 7x18/0.85 = **148 DPS** point-blank against the pistol's 22/0.15 = **147** — identical output for **4x the ammo**, from the same shared `bullets` pool, and only at contact range where the whole cone connects. Measured against the real bot scorer: the shotgun went from winning **45 of 45** sampled (profile x distance x HP) matchups to **6 of 45**. Fixed in the same change by taking `damagePerPellet` **18 -> 25** (~206 DPS, ~1.4x the pistol), so "devastating up close" survives the cadence. **The cadence and the damage are a tuned pair — retuning either means re-checking the ratio.**
  - **Two deliberate properties, now pinned by tests**: the cooldown lives on `PlayerState`, not the weapon, so tapping `1` and back can't switch-cancel the pump; and quick-melee (`Space`) bypasses `updateFiring` entirely, so a player caught mid-cycle always has something to swing.
  - **Audio**: `playShotgunPump` racks twice (two `triangle` clacks at 110->60Hz and 85->48Hz through narrow bandpasses, scheduled at +0.18s and +0.34s) so the pause reads as the gun cycling, not a dropped click. Lives inside `playShotgunBlast` rather than being engine-triggered — `viewKind` is the sound identity in `audio.ts`, and WebAudio scheduling off `ctx.currentTime` is sample-accurate where a frame-boundary trigger would jitter.
  - **`WEAPONS` joined `SIMULATION_BALANCE`** (widening `computeBalanceHash`'s values `number` -> `unknown`). Weapon tuning changes how a recorded input stream plays back while leaving `astHash` intact — exactly the silent-divergence class that guard exists for, and it was explicitly deferred in its own doc comment until "one of them is ever tuned". Folded the **whole table** in, not the two constants that moved, so future weapon edits need no list maintenance. Cost: it invalidates every pre-existing replay. Measured on the shipped board before regenerating — Casual 367 `fireQueued` frames / 117 closer than 0.15s, Gamer 324/107, **Pro 639/355 (median gap 0.058s)** — so those runs genuinely no longer reproduce, and refusing them is correct. **`defaultHighscore.ts` regenerated on the capped bot the same day**: Casual **47211**/11 levels, Gamer **55544**/11, Pro **56786**/12 — against the previous 48595/11, 50119/11, 69129/14. 86 generator checks passing, 0 failures, `PROFILES_HASH` `c7ae93fe` matching `profilesHash()`, still `gz1:`-compressed at 777KB (not the 84MB regression). **Pro's -17.9% and 14 -> 12 levels is the change's real cost showing up**, not noise on top of it: fights take ~14% longer for Pro, so it reaches the late campaign less often. Gamer's +10.8% is the same bimodality the previous regeneration documented (it has now come out 14/11/14/11/11 across five regenerations spanning 48774-64469) — the board is a max-over-qualifying-runs and remains a poor instrument for judging a code change.

- [x] **`defaultHighscore.ts` regenerated on the current bot, and the staleness guard is now ARMED (2026-08-01).** Board: Casual **48595**/11 levels, Gamer **50119**/11, Pro **69129**/14 — against the previous 46635/11, 64469/14, 52863/11. All three replays contiguous (12/12/15 segments), every segment carrying both hashes, worst level 17574 frames against the 21600 cap, 90 generator checks passing with no failures. `PROFILES_HASH` is baked in and matches `profilesHash()`, verified both ways: the test passes against the fresh file, and changing `ammoThrift` 0.2 -> 0.21 makes it fail with "re-run generate:default-highscore". **Pro's 69129 over 14 levels is the best Pro result ever recorded here** (previous best 66336, and that was before the Elite HP halving). **Gamer's -14350 is not a regression, and the whole board is a poor instrument for judging one.** The kept entry is a *maximum over 6 qualifying runs*, and Gamer's outcome is bimodal: it either gets through `stage12_render_engine.cpp` (the 2200 HP Elite level) and lands ~14 levels, or it does not and lands 11. Across four regenerations Gamer has now come out 14/11/14/11 spanning 48774-64469, and the split does not track code quality — run2 (before today's fixes) and this run (after a measured 12% combat improvement) both came out at 11. The evidence for the re-aim dodge is the n=20/side single-variable A/B, not this. Worth noting the improved bot did go deeper than any kept entry shows: one Gamer attempt reached **level 13** before getting stuck

- [ ] **`defaultHighscore.ts` staleness is now detectable — but the guard ARMS at the next regeneration (2026-08-01).** The file is generated by playing the campaign with the bot's skill profiles, so any profile retune leaves it describing a bot that no longer exists — and nothing detected that: `astHash` covers the parsed source, `balanceHash` covers the enemy roster, neither knows anything about bot tuning, so every replay stays valid while the board silently misrepresents the bot. `profilesHash()` (`scripts/lib/profiles.mjs`) fingerprints all three profiles; the generator bakes it into the file it writes as `PROFILES_HASH`, and `profiles.test.mjs` recomputes and compares. Sorted keys, so reordering fields inside a profile (no behaviour change) does not trip it; profile *order* is preserved, because `curateMixedProfiles` reads key order as tier order. Verified by injecting the correct hash (passes), changing `ammoThrift` 0.2 -> 0.25 (fails with "re-run generate:default-highscore"), and restoring. **Currently in its unarmed state**: the shipped file predates the guard, so there is no baked hash to compare and the test takes a documented no-op path rather than failing — an assertion that cannot pass until an unrelated ~35-minute job runs would simply get disabled by whoever hit it. **It arms the moment `npm run generate:default-highscore` is next run**, which is already outstanding: 7 behaviour-changing commits have landed since the last regeneration, including the re-aim dodge (a ~12% combat swing), and the profiles themselves changed in Stage 2

- [x] **Dodging now works while re-aiming, and it is the biggest single combat win measured on this branch (2026-08-01).** User's observation, and it was right: *"just because the bot is dodging doesn't mean he should stop shooting — no human would stop shooting while dodging."* Reading the code showed the bot *does* keep firing while it dodges (the dodge block sits in the same `else` arm that sets `fire`), but there was exactly **one** dodge call site and it was in that fire branch — so a bolt was only ever evaded once the bot already had a firing solution (`|delta| <= fireAngleEps` **and** LOS). The module's own note puts the fire branch at "roughly 43% of combat decisions", so for the other ~57%, while re-aiming, the bot had **no evasion at all** and was simply a stationary target. Dodging was gated behind shooting. A directed dodge now also runs in the re-aim branch: it keeps the turn key (still converging on the shot) and adds the sidestep, and drops `KeyW` for the duration because forward + lateral engages `diagonalScale` and cuts the forward axis 29% — the 0%->72% regression mechanism. **Measured, single-variable (`DODGE_WHILE_REAIMING`), Gamer/normal, n=20/side:** `enemyAccuracy` **-12.2%** (0.564 -> 0.495), `dmg.enemyRangedPerSec` **-11.4%**, `dmg.enemyRanged` -13.8%, `minHealthReached` **+27.8%** (49.2 -> 62.9), `ttkNormal` -12.4%, `levelTimeSec` -2.7%, qualifyRate 18/20 -> 19/20 with level-1 deaths halved; guards pass and route metrics flat. **This corrects the Stage 3 conclusion.** "The dodge machinery may not be net-protective" was wrong — it is strongly protective, it was just unavailable in the majority of combat decisions, which is also why per-tier dodge *quality* tuning had so little to act on. **Distinct from the reverted "strafe only once aimed"**: that added *blind* oscillation to re-aiming and perturbed heading convergence every decision (-30pp qualifyRate); this triggers only on a genuinely inbound bolt. **Caveats:** measured on Gamer/normal only — wants the other combos before the full gate; and the `stall` anomaly rate rose sharply (24.9 -> 47.3 ticks/1k decisions, 9.0 -> 14.8 findings/run), which given every outcome metric improved is more likely the detector counting an actively-dodging bot as motionless (the same class as the spike-wait false positives) than a real wedge — worth checking with `activity`-tagged findings before trusting it either way

- [ ] **Profile retune Stage 3 (per-profile evasion) MEASURED AND REVERTED — and it raises a prior question (2026-08-01).** Full write-up in `doc/dev/decisions.md`. Headline: promoting dodging/strafing to per-profile did **not** un-invert the two damage-avoidance axes, and `ttkNormal`'s smallest step fell 1.161x -> 1.118x, breaching its pre-registered 1.15x floor — revert, not tune forward, per the standing rule. The one thing that worked was the new deterministic `DODGE_REACTION_FLOOR_SEC` on the bottom tier: Casual's `enemyAccuracy` rose 11% (0.499 -> 0.556). **Root cause found by reading, and it corrects what this entry first said.** The initial reading — "dodging may not be net-protective" — was wrong. The bot *does* keep shooting while it dodges (the dodge block is inside the same `else` arm that sets `fire`). What it cannot do is dodge while **re-aiming**: there is exactly one dodge call site and it lives in the fire branch, so a bolt is only dodged when the bot already has a firing solution (`|delta| <= fireAngleEps` and LOS). The module's own comment puts the fire branch at "roughly 43% of combat decisions" — so in the other ~57% the bot has no evasion available at all and is simply a stationary target. Tuning dodge quality per tier therefore had almost nothing to act on. **Next experiment: allow the *directed* dodge in the re-aim branch.** Distinct from the already-reverted "strafe only once aimed" (that added *blind* oscillation to re-aiming and fought aim convergence every decision, -30pp qualifyRate); a directed dodge triggers only on a genuinely inbound bolt. Must drop `KeyW` while dodging — forward + lateral engages `diagonalScale` and cuts the forward axis 29%, the 0%->72% regression mechanism. **Open attribution gap I should close first:** Stage 2 was only measured at Tier-1 (n=8, 4 levels), so the ttk drop from the n=40 baseline's 1.161x cannot be cleanly split between Stage 2 and Stage 3. The committed state is Stage 2 and its Tier-2 number is unknown — a 3-profile n=20 run (~35 min) settles whether what is currently on the branch clears its own bar

- [ ] **Profile retune Stage 2: knob signs fixed, ladder grader built (2026-08-01).** `ammoThrift` was 1.6/0.4/0.9 and `selfHarmAversion` 2.2/0.9/1.3 — both non-monotonic, i.e. Gamer was the *most* spendthrift and Pro the *more* self-harm-averse of the two. Now 1.6/0.4/**0.2** and 2.2/0.9/**0.5**, with Gamer anchored (it is pinned by `run-perf-benchmark.mjs`) and Pro moved, per the "widen both ends, hold the middle" decision. Casual also loses ghidra from `weaponPriority` — membership is a hard filter, so that is a real behavioural tier (a casual player never reaches for the rocket launcher) rather than a preference. All six ladder knobs are now strictly monotonic and pinned by `profiles.test.mjs`. **New grader:** `scripts/lib/profileSeparation.mjs` + `report-profile-separation.mjs`, reusing `abReport.mjs`'s `collectMetrics` so both reports share one metric definition; exits non-zero on a failing ladder. It bounds the **smallest adjacent step**, not the whole-ladder max/min — the distinction is the whole point, because Casual is cleanly separated from both others while **Gamer↔Pro** is the step that actually flipped run-to-run at n=5, and an ends-based bar cannot fail on that. Validated against the n=40 baseline, where it reproduces the known numbers and correctly fails. **Measured after Stage 2 (Tier-1: n=8, 4 levels — sign only, not a success declaration):** `ttkNormal` smallest step **1.161x → 1.317x** (Pro 0.755 → 0.614, i.e. `ammoThrift` 0.2 buying faster kills), `levelTimeSec` holding at 1.124x, and **both damage-avoidance axes still inverted** — as expected, since those are what the evasion axis in Stage 3 exists to fix. **Bug found and fixed in the process:** Stage 0's extraction used `export { PROFILES } from "./lib/profiles.mjs"`, which re-exports *without* creating a local binding — so `run-balancing-telemetry.mjs` itself crashed with `PROFILES is not defined` at startup while every importer of it still worked, and the unit suite stayed green because tests import `profiles.mjs` directly. Nothing ran the actual script. Caught only when the first Tier-1 run exited 1 three times

- [ ] **Profile retune Stage 1 done; `rocketForDistantClusters` deliberately NOT deleted (2026-08-01).** The plan called for deleting both vestigial knobs. `coverageMode` is gone and that one was provably free: it was `false` for all three profiles, so `profile.coverageMode ? routeCoverage : routePlain` always evaluated to `routePlain` — a proof, not a measurement. It was also expensive: `planCoverageRoute` costs **1833ms against `planRoute`'s 538ms across the 17-level campaign, i.e. 77% of all route-planning time**, spent every `planLevels()` call on a route that was never read. `planCoverageRoute` itself stays in `routePlanner.mjs` (tested, real capability, now documented as having no live caller). **`rocketForDistantClusters` is deferred, because the plan's rationale for it is wrong.** The plan said deleting it hands rockets to the scoring loop "on their merits" — it does not. The knob gates one arm of a cluster bypass in `pickRangedWeapon`, and removing that arm falls through to `if (ownedWeapons.includes(1)) return shotgun` — *another* bypass, reached before the scoring loop. So for Gamer and Pro (both `true`) deleting it means rocket -> shotgun against a cluster at >=`ROCKET_CLUSTER_MIN_DIST` (5 tiles) whenever ghidra is owned with ammo and the aim is safe. That is a real behaviour change shipped under a cleanup label, and at 5+ tiles a shotgun is a poor substitute for a rocket. Options for whoever picks this up: (a) delete the whole cluster bypass so clusters really are score-selected — but `scoreRangedWeapon` scores a single target and has no cluster awareness, so that loses the behaviour rather than relocating it; (b) drop only the `profile.` condition so every tier uses rockets on distant clusters — changes Casual instead; (c) keep it and accept a knob that differentiates Casual only. Needs measuring, not a cleanup commit

- [x] **Stage 6 (pre-emptive engagement) measured and REVERTED (2026-08-01).** Full protocol: 4 combos (Casual/normal as a null control, Gamer/normal, Pro/normal, Pro/hard) x ATTEMPT_CAP=40 x 2 sides, ~3h, single-variable via `PREEMPTIVE_ENGAGE` against one dev server. **Guards passed on all four and all three stage-specific rollbacks were clear** (`combatVsExplorationRatio` max +2.4% against a +20% limit, `peakSimultaneousAggroed` never rose, `timeAtZeroRangedAmmoSec` 0 -> 0 everywhere) — and it still does not earn its place, because it changes nothing measurable. The null control is what makes that readable: Casual is gated off *and* had the switch off on the base side, so both its sides were identical code, and it still moved `enemyAccuracy` +5.2%, `minHealthReached` -29.5%, `stall` +14.4%, `trapSpike` +57.6%. Every feature-ON movement sits inside that. The decisive metric was `killsObserved`: control +0.2% against -1.0%/+0.3%/-0.6% on the three ON combos — if the bot were starting fights it otherwise skips, kills would rise, and they do not. **Then instrumented rather than assumed** (counters on the gate, 24 levels / 35,770 decisions): the gate opens on **0.99%** of decisions and is acted on in **0.66%**, ~10 per level. Root cause is structural and is the reason not to re-attempt this shape: the gate mirrors the engine's own aggro rule, so an eligible enemy is one the engine aggros on its next AI tick — a one-tick window by construction. Making it fire more means engaging outside the aggro radius, i.e. starting fights that would not have happened, contradicting the user's standing "don't run a corridor for an enemy that can't reach me" constraint. Full write-up in `doc/dev/decisions.md`. **Kept from the exercise:** the walking-distance gate proved load-bearing (rejected 27% of candidates, and 43 of 45 on level 7 — visible across a gap), `pickThreat` now receives `this.tuning` at every call site (an omitted argument would have made this A/B's own baseline run candidate behaviour), and Pro/normal stays in the A/B combo set

- [x] **`defaultHighscore.ts` regenerated once more, on the replay fix (2026-08-01).** Board: Casual **46635**/11 levels, Gamer **64469**/14, Pro **52863**/11. All three replays now verified contiguous by the new generator guard, 12/15/12 segments, every segment carrying both hashes, worst level 17085 frames against the 21600 cap. The failure path was exercised rather than assumed: this run logged one `[replay] "demo-campaign/stage10_kernel_module.rs" exceeded 21600 frames` **and** one matching truncation warning, i.e. an attempt did overflow and the new code truncated it instead of splicing — the previous behaviour would have shipped another silent gap. Scores remain noisy (Gamer 48774-64469 across five regenerations); nothing here is a bot regression

- [x] **The skipped replay level: the frame cap, plus a `filter` that hid it (2026-08-01).** Root cause of the Casual entry whose replay ran level 9 -> level 11. Two things had to line up. (1) `MAX_REPLAY_FRAMES_PER_LEVEL` is 21600 and the generator records at `RECORD_STEP_MS` = 1/60s, so the cap is **six minutes of a single level**; Casual is the slowest profile and `stage10_kernel_module.rs` one of the longest levels, so it overflows there intermittently, `LevelRecorder.record()` sets `overflowed` and `finish()` returns `null`. (2) `CampaignReplayRecorder.finish()` then ran `resolved.filter(l => l !== null)`, which **spliced the gap out of the middle of the sequence** — correct for the trailing-empty-level case it was written for, silently wrong for a middle one. Every surviving segment stayed individually valid (astHash, balanceHash, frames all fine), so nothing downstream could notice, and `startReplay` walks segments in order and simply jumped a level. **Why I first ruled the cap out, wrongly:** the overflow warning is a browser-side `console.warn`, and the highscore generator — unlike `run-balancing-telemetry.mjs` — never forwarded page console, so `grep exceeded` on the Node log returned 0. Proving it took adding that forwarding (now `CODEENSTEIN_CONSOLE_FORWARD=1`, same opt-in shape as the telemetry script), after which the log showed exactly one line: `[replay] "demo-campaign/stage10_kernel_module.rs" exceeded 21600 frames`, on precisely the file that goes missing. Two earlier hypotheses were disproven along the way and are worth not re-attempting: it is **not** a parse failure (`advanceToNextLevel` does silently skip a file whose `parseFile` returns null — a real latent hazard, since stage10 is the campaign's only `.rs` file — but the console shows zero `[parser] Skipping` lines and `[level] ... advancing to stage10_kernel_module.rs` firing on every attempt), and it is **not** `MAX_REPLAY_LEVELS` (that truncates the tail). **Fix:** `finish()` now truncates at the first unsavable level instead of filtering, so a payload is always a contiguous prefix of the run, and warns when it does. Plus a generator-side guard that asserts each kept entry's replay is a contiguous campaign prefix and refuses to write otherwise — verified against the shipped file, where it fails Casual with `got [... stage09, stage11, stage12]` and blocks the write. Both new unit tests confirmed red against the old `filter`

- [x] **`defaultHighscore.ts` regenerated after the bot fixes; the recorder bug found while doing it is now root-caused and fixed (2026-07-31, closed 2026-08-01 — see the entry above).** Regenerated on `f53de93` (nearest-key route ordering + loot-detour re-planning). Kept board: Casual **48884**/11 levels, Gamer **51945**/11, Pro **56347**/12 — against the previous 48076/11, 49506/11, 66336/14. **Do not read Pro's -15% as a regression:** the kept entry is a *maximum* over qualifying runs, and across four whole regenerations the same profile ranged 51059-61480 (11-12 levels) while Gamer ranged 48774-63089 (11-14). Run-to-run noise is comparable to the gap between any two boards, and the old Pro entry (66336/14) was itself a lucky tail. Generated with a larger sample than the default at the user's direction — new `CODEENSTEIN_HIGHSCORE_QUALIFYING_RUNS` env var (default unchanged at 3): the loop stops the moment N runs qualify, so at the default a profile used only **4 of its 40 permitted attempts** and the board was a best-of-3. At 6 each profile used 8 attempts. **Open bug found in the process:** the kept Casual entry's replay is missing its `stage10_kernel_module.rs` segment — 11 segments for the 12 levels the run actually played, so playback jumps from level 9 to level 11. It is specific to **Casual + stage10 and reproduced in 2 of 3 regenerations** (the third was clean, so it is luck, not determinism); Gamer and Pro record that level correctly every time. Not caused by today's changes. Playback is not broken — `startReplay` walks `payload.levels` sequentially and validates each segment's own `astHash`/`balanceHash`, all of which are present and correct — the level is simply absent. `ReplayRecorder.finish()` returns `null` both on frame-cap overflow and when a segment "never captured a single frame"; the cap is not it (zero `exceeded` warnings, worst level 14701 frames against a cap of 21600), so the zero-frame path is the suspect. Note stage10 is the level whose exit stays inert while an exit-room enemy lives, which used to fail ~58% of Casual attempts before `driveToExit` — probably the same neighbourhood

- [x] **Loot detours now re-plan like route legs — the dominant wedge class in the scan (2026-07-31).** Root-caused by adding one field to the anomaly detail line: `activity`, which the trace already recorded and nothing printed. `branch` was useless (282 of 283 findings are `main`); `activity` split the same findings instantly — **9 of 13 idle stalls were `activity=loot`**, including both clusters I had previously attributed to door opening. The bot was never opening those doors: it walks *past* the doorway on a loot detour, and `maybeDetourForLoot` drove its waypoints with the bare straight-line `driveToward` while route legs had long since moved to `driveTowardWithReplan`. Shoved off the line, the straight line aims into the door frame for the whole 600-tick budget; the engine rejects the blocked axis and slides the free one, so **y stays pinned while x crawls** — `stage03_legacy_api.php` y=70.5 (door at y=71), x 23.53 -> 23.69 over 254 decisions. `driveTowardWithReplan`'s own doc comment describes this exact failure, captured at (38.8,56.3) on `stage06_pipeline.py`; the fix simply never reached this one call site. One-line change. **Measured across six scans:** the two clusters appear in **3 of 4 pre-fix runs** (4+6, 3+6, 4+3) and are **0 in both post-fix runs**; idle loot stalls **9 -> 0 -> 0**; qualifying **3/3 on all three combos throughout** (the guard that caught the previous attempt). Totals drift in both directions run to run (stalls 112-160 across runs) — the class-level numbers are the signal, not the totals. **Correction to the earlier entry above:** the door-staging observation is real and stays true — the staging point sits 0.15 tiles outside `openDoorAhead`'s reach for all four approach directions — but it was **not** the cause of these stalls, and the follow-up it proposed (verify the push opened the door, retry if not) was implemented and **measured to be a no-op**: across a full scan the retry path fired **zero** times, i.e. `getGrid` reported the door open after the first push every time. That hypothesis is dead; the code was reverted rather than kept as speculative recovery for a failure that does not occur

- [x] **"Door-approach" stalls were loot detours, and are fixed (2026-07-31).** **The door framing was wrong** — see the correction at the end of this entry. Chasing the residual idle stalls from the classification pass. **First correction: they are not 4 and 6 separate stalls, they are one continuous episode each.** The L3 findings are back-to-back decision ranges (1582-1658, 1659-1735, 1736-1812, 1813-1835) with **y pinned at 70.51** while x creeps +0.05 at a time; the detector split one ~254-decision episode into four because each 0.05 drift exceeded `TRACE_POS_EPS`. Same shape on L7 (492-661). Reading the four as independent events is what made them look like a frequency problem rather than one wedge. **Proven defect:** `openDoorAhead` (engine.ts) probes from the player's *exact* position `radius + 0.15 = 0.35` tiles along its facing, and the tile that probe lands in must BE the door tile; the bot's staging point is the plain centre of the tile in front of the door, **0.5 tiles out — 0.15 beyond the reach, for all four approach directions** (verified arithmetically for each). So the door has *never* opened on arrival at the staging point; it only ever opens because `holdForwardFine` walks the bot forward afterwards, which makes that nudge load-bearing rather than a safety margin. **Attempted fix, REVERTED:** move the staging point into the openable band. It regressed **qualifying runs 3/3 -> 0/3 on all three profiles** — the scan also ran 3.5x faster with 6x fewer findings, which is the tell, since fewer findings there meant a smaller denominator (runs dying early), not better behaviour. Cause of the regression is a genuine conflict: the band is only **0.15 tiles wide** (0.2 collision floor to 0.35 reach ceiling) while **one walk decision is 0.16 tiles**, and wall collision rejects an entire step rather than clamping it — so the bot cannot reliably land inside the band by coarse driving, and now got stranded *approaching* the staging point instead of at it. Revert verified: 3/3 restored. **Next step is the push, not the staging** — the coarse-approach-then-fine-push design is right (`holdForwardFine`'s doc comment already reasons about exactly this collision behaviour), so the fix belongs in making the push verifiably succeed: the bot now has `getGrid`/`getGridVersion` hooks, so it can *check whether the door actually opened* after pushing and retry, instead of assuming it did and walking into a replan loop when it didn't. Not attempted yet. **Still unexplained and worth reading first:** during the episode the bot drifts in **x while y stays constant**, i.e. it is holding forward while *not* facing the door — so `faceAngle` may not have converged, and note it falls through to full combat whenever `pickThreat` returns anything (this episode logged `threatDist=none` throughout, so that path alone does not explain it)

- [x] **The 70 "idle" stalls classified: ~72% were the anomaly detector calling correct spike-trap waiting a freeze (2026-07-31).** The scan report's honest-remainder figure (162 stalls, 92 with a threat in range = correct hold-and-fire, **70 idle**) turned out to be almost entirely a detector artifact. Three independent lines of evidence, none of them an aggregate over the detector's own findings: (1) **geometry** — 98.5% of idle stalls fall into just 14 repeat tiles, and **13 of those 14 sit immediately adjacent to a spike trap**; 11 of 14 freeze all three profiles at the same tile, which is map geometry, not tuning; (2) **tick-level trace** — 9 of 9 traced runs were **88-98% spike-blocked** with ~95% of ticks holding *no movement key at all*, i.e. the bot deliberately standing still; (3) **timing** — a trap is up for half its period, and the campaign's 45 traps give a blocked phase of **36-55 ticks**, which is exactly where the idle-stall durations land (median 34, p90 47, **max 55**). The bot waits one phase and never longer; it cannot do better without taking damage. **Mechanism:** the exclusion was `runSlice.every((r) => r.waitingOnSpike)`, but `waitingOnSpike` is computed from a probe 0.6 tiles ahead *along the current facing* (`combatPolicy.mjs:1698`), so it blinks off for a tick or two whenever the bot adjusts heading mid-wait — one such tick billed an entirely correct 34-tick wait as a freeze. Replaced with a dominance test at **0.8**, deliberately higher than the `mostlyFiring`/`mostlyEngaged` guards' 0.5 beside it: a bot genuinely *wedged* next to a trap measures ~50% (the trap keeps cycling underneath it), while a real one-phase wait never dilutes below ~0.88, so the high bar is what preserves the detector's ability to still catch a wedge next to a spike — pinned by a test both ways. Matched re-scan: **idle stalls 68 -> 19**. Caveat stated plainly: the two scans are separate stochastic runs (threat-present stalls moved 107 -> 141 on variance alone), so the count is directional, not exact — the tick-level and timing evidence is what carries the conclusion. Also fixed the trace dump to emit the longest run of *each* finding type per level rather than the longest overall, which is why only 1 of 5 idle stalls had rows on the first attempt. **The honest remainder is now ~2 real clusters, and they are a new class: door-approach stalls.** `stage03_legacy_api.php` (23.6,70.5) freezes **77 ticks, four times, identically** — above the 55-tick spike ceiling, nearest spike 12.9 tiles away, and **0.78 tiles from locked door (23,71)**; `stage07_service.rb` (70.6,22.5) freezes 38 ticks six times, one tile below branch door (70,21). Both Pro/normal in this sample. Not investigated further — this is the next thing to pull on, and it is a far smaller and better-defined target than "70 idle stalls"

- [x] **Level 3's key/door chain shortened — the level design was never the problem, the bot's pickup ordering was (2026-07-31).** The scan report flagged `stage03_legacy_api.php` as the campaign's distorting outlier (153-188s vs 29-103s, 24% of all findings, only 23 enemies) and recommended shortening the chain. Measuring first said otherwise. Level 6 has **7 doors and 7 keys and plans in a single leg** at 1.05x its shortest path, while level 3 has 8 door tiles / 4 gates and plans 13 legs at 3.73x — so gate *count* is not the cost. The cost was the order: `planRoute` collects every reachable key before opening the next gate, and it took them in `map.keys` array order, which is `placeKeys`' push order (gate-opening order) and says nothing about where the player stands. On level 3 that walked 94 tiles west to one key, **85 tiles back east to a key sitting 7 tiles from spawn**, then 114 west again to the first gate — 293 waypoints to do about 130 waypoints of work, all before the first door opened. `routePlanner.mjs`'s own doc comment had claimed "nearest reachable uncollected key" since it was written; only now is that true. Safe by construction: since the branch runs until no reachable key is left, the *set* collected before each gate is identical either way, so solvability cannot change — unlike the **door** order, which stays array-order deliberately (tile-order scanning deadlocked `stage13_batch_job.scala`, see `findReachableDoor`) and is now pinned by a test. Measured over the full 17-level campaign, planned route length: **level 3 529 -> 401 waypoints (-24%, 3.73x -> 2.82x)**, level 10 538 -> 426 (-21%), and **level 17 `the_monolith` 4610 -> 2924 (-37%)** — 51 keys, the biggest win and not something the report had flagged. Total **-25.3%**; every other level byte-identical, no plan failures, no `crossesHazard` changes, deterministic across repeated runs. First unit tests `routePlanner.mjs` has ever had (`routePlanner.test.mjs`, 4 tests, CI-wired via the `scripts/**.test.mjs` precedent); the nearest-key test was confirmed red against the pre-fix planner (`expected '10,1' to be '3,1'`), the other three pin invariants that must *not* move. **Route length is measured exactly; wall-clock level time is not** — that needs a `balancing:scan` to confirm, since level time also carries combat and stalls. An earlier attempt at the report's literal recommendation — biasing key *placement* in `src/map/generation/doorsKeys.ts` toward the gate each key opens — was measured and **reverted**: it moved level 3 only -2.6% and made level 7 **24% worse**, because placing a key near its gate ignores which side the player approaches from

- [x] **Pro/normal level-1 wedge root-caused and fixed (2026-07-31).** ~22s frozen at `(60.26,57.35)` in roughly 2 of 5 attempts. **Mechanism:** `#walkPathTo` BFSes with `LOOT_DETOUR_AVOID_TILES` (spikes + acid). From that tile on `main.c` the exit is **95 tiles away by plain BFS but NULL once spikes and acid are excluded** — every route crosses one. A null BFS returns `{state:"playing", reason:"stuck"}`, which `driveToExit` does *not* treat as fatal, so it fell through to `driveToward` — which steers a straight line with no pathfinding — and sprinted into a wall for the full `FINAL_APPROACH_TICKS` (80) budget, `EXIT_CLEAR_ROUNDS` (6) times over. 6x80=480 ticks, matching the 438-halt observed. The trace is unmistakable: 438 identical decisions, `delta=+0.001` (perfectly aimed), `navDist=52.74` (the exit, straight-line), `keys=[KeyW,ShiftLeft]`, position never changing. **Fix:** `pathAvoidingHazardsIfPossible` — strict avoid-set first, then retry allowing hazard terrain, the same two-tier shape `driveTowardWithReplan` already uses for doors. Refusing to plan is only correct when a better plan exists; that is true for an *optional* loot detour and false for reaching the exit. **Measured, n=16 Pro/normal attempts, single-variable via `BOT_HAZARD_ROUTE_FALLBACK`:** long stalls (>=200 ticks) **4 -> 0**, longest stall **441 -> 61**, exit-inert rounds **24 -> 4** (without the fix `driveToExit` burns all six rounds every time; with it the first resolves). The fallback fired 4 times in the fix-on run — i.e. the path under test actually executed. Also extended `TRACE_DUMP` to dump the longest finding of *any* type, not just oscillation: this wedge is a `stall`, so the oscillation-only dump captured nothing of it


- [x] **Balancing scan + highscore regeneration after the bot rework (2026-07-31 04:22).** Report: https://claude.ai/code/artifact/c21742a1-6d52-4dc1-9778-e19359a8adf9 — findings, interpretation and recommendations, incl. the profile-intent analysis. Scan at `8ce3f4b`: 233 findings (162 stall / 64 healthDrainFrozen / 7 oscillation), no crashes, no new anomaly class; Casual 5/5, Gamer 4/5, Pro 2/5 qualifying. **Headline: Pro/normal freezes ~22s at a fixed tile on level 1 in 2 of 5 attempts** — reproduced with all four of today's switches *disabled*, so it is pre-existing, not a regression; it went unseen because every A/B today ran Pro on *hard*. Level 3 remains the campaign's distorting outlier (153-188s vs 29-103s, 24% of all findings, only 23 enemies — the time is traversal). **Profiles: Casual is cleanly differentiated; Gamer and Pro have collapsed together**, and Pro is *worse* on TTK (0.95 vs 0.92) and route overhead (1.817 vs 1.799). Likely cause is ours: `rotSpeedMultiplier` (2/3.5/5) was a sharp differentiator *because the bot stood still to turn*, and since `ee7a658` it no longer does — the stop-to-turn A/B shows the compression directly (Casual gained 22.4% level time, Pro only 8.3%). Suggested: widen `fireCooldownMs`/`fireAngleEps`/`healthDetourThreshold` between Gamer and Pro instead of turn speed, and check whether Pro's `healthDetourThreshold: 0.25` is still justified. Caveat throughout: n=5, one difficulty — ordering is visible, tuning values are not


- [x] **`driveToExit` ranks exit blockers by walking distance now, and the combined "act on accurate information" A/B is settled (2026-07-31).** `driveToExit` sorted exit-room enemies by `Math.hypot` and hunted the nearest, so an enemy 5 tiles away through a wall (20 by corridor) was hunted before one 8 tiles down an open corridor — with a bounded per-round budget, that could burn the budget and report `stuck`. Replaced by `rankExitBlockers`, a pure exported helper ranking on BFS length over the now-live grid, using the same avoid-set `#walkPathTo` actually walks with; unreachable blockers sort *last* rather than being dropped (the exit stays inert while any homed enemy lives, so discarding one turns a hunt into a silent `stuck`), index breaks ties. **Combined A/B** (this + `BOT_LIVE_GRID`, n=12/combo/side): `qualifyRate` **+8.3pp on 2 of 3** (0.917 -> 1.000, flat on Pro), `ttkNormal` better 3/3, guards pass 3/3 — against `routeFollowOverhead`/`levelTimeSec` **up 3/3** (+2.5/+9.7/+6.4%). Coverage, kills and secret rooms all flat, so it is not visiting more of the map. **Attributed with `summarizeActivityDistance` rather than another A/B:** of the extra distance, **53% is `loot`** (+5.5t/level — pickups it previously believed were walled off; `pctRegularKillLootMisses` fell 5.2%/21.4% on Casual/Gamer), 41% is `route` (+1.8%, no mechanism identified — the honest open bit), and **`exitHunt` went 0.1 -> 0.0 t/level, i.e. the blocker change costs nothing measurable** — which isolates the cost to `BOT_LIVE_GRID` for free. Kept on that basis: the dominant cost is the bot succeeding at retrieval it used to fail, and the blocker fix is free. Magnitude is noisy between runs (+9.7% in the A/B vs +3.4% in the attribution run, same combo, same sign) — the attribution is trustworthy, the size is not


- [ ] **Firefox may be un-skippable in CI after all — one untried pref (`media.peerconnection.ice.loopback`).** `verify-multiplayer-connect.mjs` is skipped for the `firefox` leg of the `verify-browser` matrix (`if: matrix.browser != 'firefox'`) because Firefox gathers **zero** ICE candidates on GitHub Actions: it finds its default-route interface by `connect()`-ing a UDP socket toward a public IP purely to ask the OS which interface would be used, and the runner has no internet-routable default route, so gathering yields nothing (Mozilla bug 1659672, closed RESOLVED INVALID). That diagnosis is solid; the conclusion "therefore unfixable" is the part that may not follow. The script's own comment lists what was ruled out — `obfuscate_host_addresses` (kept, different quirk), fake `getUserMedia` (kept, wrong restriction), `network.dns.disableIPv6` (no effect), `force_interface` (needs the runner's interface name, not discoverable) — and **`media.peerconnection.ice.loopback` is not among them.** That pref lets Firefox gather candidates on the loopback interface, which is precisely what this case needs: both peers are two contexts in *one* Playwright process on *one* machine, so loopback candidates are sufficient and no routable interface is required at all. It is what Mozilla's own WebRTC tests use for this. **Cheap to test.** ~~This dev box is a good stand-in for the runner — outbound UDP is blocked here too~~ — **that premise is dead (2026-08-03): UDP egress works here and Chromium multiplayer passes locally, so this box is *not* a stand-in for the runner's no-routable-default-route shape.** A local Firefox pass would therefore be weaker evidence than the original note assumed, and a local Firefox *failure* would be strong evidence (it would mean the pref doesn't help even with working egress). Still worth the ten minutes, just read the result the right way round. Test: add the pref to `FIREFOX_LAUNCH_OPTIONS`, run `CODEENSTEIN_VERIFY_BROWSER=firefox npm run verify:multiplayer-connect`, check `installIceDiagnostics()` reports candidates. If it works: drop the matrix skip and correct `doc/dev/testing.md` again. Caveats: untested, and Firefox pref names/behaviour drift between versions


- [x] **Bot now sees the engine's live wall grid — the stale-door class is fixed at the source (2026-07-31).** The engine mutates its own grid mid-level (a door opens, a secret wall floods away) and bumps `gridVersion`; the bot planned forever against the *copy* it was handed at level start, so an opened door stayed a wall in every BFS it ran. That is the recorded `stage03_legacy_api.php` wedge, and `#noteDoorUnderFoot` exists only as a partial workaround — it can learn about a door the player personally stands on, never one that opened as a side effect or that a *teammate* opened. Found by auditing the engine's capability surface against what the bot actually calls: `PathField` (`src/engine/pathField.ts`) has been maintaining a live, grid-mutation-aware BFS distance field the whole time, and `scripts/lib/` had **zero** references to `gridVersion` or any grid hook. New `getGridVersion()`/`getGrid()` test hooks (grid copied row-by-row, never a live ref — `PathField` floods over that exact array); the bot folds the version into the read it already makes each decision and refetches the grid only when it moves. Verified end to end rather than by outcome alone: a unit test drives a real branch-door open and asserts the version bumps and the tile flips through the hooks, and a play run logged **8 grid refreshes** — i.e. the path actually executed. Gated by `BOT_LIVE_GRID` so it stays single-variable A/B-able. **Behaviourally unmeasured so far** — it changes what the bot's pathfinding believes, so it wants an A/B before being called a win. Unblocks the two walking-distance items below


- [x] **Stage 7 (backpedal retreat) shipped 2026-07-31 — the last movement stage of the rework plan.** `criticalHealth` used to spin to face away from a threat while holding `KeyW`, so for the first half of a large turn it *ran toward the thing chasing it* — the identical defect measured in the mine retreat. It now runs along the escape vector immediately via the 8-way pick, no turn, which also keeps the threat in view (a precondition for firing while retreating; firing itself deliberately left out as a separate variable). Full protocol, n=12/combo/side, single-variable via `NAV_BACKPEDAL_RETREAT`: `enemyAccuracy` **-3.5/-5.3/-3.6%** and `dmg.enemyRanged` **-4.9/-1.2/-2.9%** on 3/3 — both metrics the change targets; `qualifyRate` 1.000/**0.917->1.000**/1.000, and **zero deaths on the candidate side of all three combos** (Gamer's lone level-1 death, worth its whole 8.3pp, disappeared). Guards pass 3/3 and the stage's own tightened rollback (qualifyRate down >8pp, conditionalDeathRate up >10pp) is not breached anywhere. **Costs, honestly:** `ttkNormal` worse on 3/3 (+1.5/+4.8/+5.2%), plausibly because a bot that disengages properly stretches fights out — arguably correct at critical health, but a real cost. `minHealthReached` mixed (Casual -21.1% against Gamer +31.5%, Pro +15.1%) and weighted weakly: it is a *minimum*, so one unlucky run moves it, and Casual had zero deaths on both sides. On `diagonalScale`: the 72% regression came from bolting a lateral key onto a *forward* move, leaving the resultant 45deg off the escape line at 71% efficiency; here the octant is chosen to point along the escape vector, so worst case is half an octant (22.5deg, 92%), and a pure-lateral escape has no `diagonalScale` factor at all


- [ ] **Walking-distance audit (2026-07-31): two sites use straight-line distance where travel distance is meant.** Prompted by the Stage 6 constraint; the sweep found the problem is *narrow*, not general. **(1) `driveToExit`'s blocker choice (`bot.mjs:1068`) is wrong today, in shipped code** — it sorts exit-room enemies by `Math.hypot` and hunts the nearest, so an enemy 5 tiles away through a wall (20 by corridor) is hunted before one 8 tiles down an open corridor. **(2) `pickThreat` (`combatPolicy.mjs:705`)** — the Stage 6 issue below. **Correct as-is, deliberately:** mine blast radius / disarm range and `visibleMineNear` (blast geometry is euclidean), rocket `CLUSTER_RADIUS`, `ARRIVE_EPS`, `LEG_REPLAN_DRIFT_TILES`, `TELEPORT_JUMP_DETECT_TILES`, distance to the current waypoint (~1 tile, in LOS), and the anomaly detectors' path/net measurements — all physical, not travel. **Loot detours already do the right thing:** the `hypot` at `bot.mjs:814` is only a prefilter and is conservative-safe (straight-line <= walking distance, so it can only reject genuinely-too-far items), after which it ranks on a real `bfsPath` length and re-checks the cap against that. Its remaining exposure is the *stale grid*, not the measure — it BFSes the bot's static map copy plus the hand-maintained `openedDoors` set, which the grid-exposure work fixes

- [ ] **Stage 6 (pre-emptive engagement) — build it against WALKING distance, not straight-line.** `pickThreat` filters `e.alive && e.aggroed`, so the bot walks past enemies it can plainly see; Stage 6 relaxes that. **User constraint (2026-07-31), and it is the crux of the stage:** *only engage enemies that are a realistic threat — do not run a long corridor for an enemy that has no chance of aggroing the player.* Note `pickThreat` today ranks and gates on `Math.hypot` straight-line distance, so an enemy 7 tiles away through a wall but 25 tiles by corridor currently reads as "close". Relaxing the aggro filter on top of a straight-line gate would send the bot on exactly the detours the user is describing. The plan's own gate (LOS required, `dist <= AGGRO_RADIUS` 7.5 rather than `engageRadius` 9.5, forward hemisphere, profile-gated) is necessary but not sufficient — LOS plus a short straight line still admits an enemy across an unreachable gap. **The right measure exists already:** `PathField.distAt(x,y)` is a live BFS distance from the player's tile through the *current* grid (`src/engine/pathField.ts`), which is exactly "how far would I actually have to walk to reach it". If the PathField/grid-exposure work lands first, Stage 6 should gate on that rather than on `hypot`. Rollback thresholds for the stage are already tightened in the plan (`combatVsExplorationRatio` up >20%, `peakSimultaneousAggroed.max` up >=2, any rise in `desperation.timeAtZeroRangedAmmoSec`) and it wants `ATTEMPT_CAP=40` from the start, since its sign is unknown a priori and aggro is sticky and irreversible


- [x] **Bot stop-to-turn removed — the biggest measured win of the rework (2026-07-31).** The bot has always had full WASD and only ever walked forwards: at every route corner past `MAX_WALK_WHILE_TURNING_RAD` (0.35 rad) it added *only* a turn key and stood still until the heading converged. `planRoute` emits one waypoint per tile, so a winding corridor presents that every tile — measured **385 corners across the 8 demo levels, 1585 frozen decisions, 79.3s, ~13.4% of level time** (23.5% of all decisions were turn-only). **The enabling fact, which nobody had checked:** `moveForward` translates along `(dirX,dirY)` and `strafe` along `(-dirY,dirX)`, both scaled by the same `step`, with `diagonalScale` (1/sqrt(2)) applied per axis when both are held — so two perpendicular components have magnitude *exactly* `step`. Eight directions, all at full speed, sprint included, no turn required; worst-case direction error is half an octant (22.5deg, cos = 92%) versus 0% while frozen. Now picks the 8-way combination pointing at the target and keeps moving while it turns; safety checks scan along the **movement** vector rather than facing (`movementVectorFor`), which they never had to before. Full protocol, n=12/combo/side, single-variable via `CODEENSTEIN_TELEMETRY_TUNING='{"NAV_FULL_WASD":false}'`:

| combo | levelTimeSec | routeFollowOverhead | qualifyRate | minHealthReached | guards |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| Casual/normal | **-22.4%** | -1.1% | 1.000 -> 1.000 | +4.3% | pass |
| Gamer/normal | **-15.5%** | -4.4% | 0.917 -> 0.917 | +2.6% | pass |
| Pro/hard | **-8.3%** | +0.7% | 0.917 -> **1.000** | +315% (9.1 -> 38.0) | pass |

Frozen decisions 23.5% -> 1.4%; `healthDrainFrozen` **halved on all three** (-48 to -51%) — that anomaly largely *was* the freeze, the bot standing motionless while taking damage. `enemyAccuracy` down on all three, `ttkNormal` down or flat. **Costs, small but consistently signed:** hazard damage up on 3/3 (0.076->0.290, 0.031->0.050, 0->0.045) — the predicted risk of strafing into terrain a facing-scan would not have caught; absolutes are a fraction of a HP per level and `minHealthReached` rose everywhere, so it is not costing survival, but the direction is consistent. `stall` ticks up on 2 of 3 (+28/+18/-3.5%). Oscillation moves are noise at 0.5-1.0 findings/run. **Every pacing number this bot has ever reported was inflated by the standing around** — treat pre-2026-07-31 `levelTimeSec` and `combatVsExplorationRatio` as biased


- [ ] **Bot mine retreat now backpedals instead of turning around — logic verified, real-world effect UNMEASURED (2026-07-31).** `mineRetreat` used to spin to face away from the mine while holding `KeyW`, so a retreat that began facing the mine (the normal case — you walk into one before noticing) *advanced on it* while turning: captured on `stage03_legacy_api.php` moving 7.86 -> 7.41, i.e. 0.45 tiles closer, mine 2.5 -> 2.2, over five decisions. That wasted motion is what made the retreat unable to finish: `findDangerousMine` fires at `MINE_BLAST_RADIUS + reactionBuffer` (2.4 + 0.32 = **2.72**) and `findDisarmableMine` only starts *above* it, but the bot never got past **2.6** before burning all `MINE_TARGET_GIVEUP_TICKS` (40 — exactly the count observed) and abandoning the mine. So it could neither pass nor shoot it. **The fix is the user's observation, not the original one:** the bot never needed to turn at all. `moveForward` takes a signed step (`forwardSign -= 1` for `KeyS`, engine.ts) so reversing is the same speed as advancing, sprint included — and backing up keeps the bot *facing* the mine, so the disarm shot is available the instant it clears the blast radius rather than requiring a second 180. **The bot had never dispatched `KeyS` anywhere in its history**; the whole backward axis was unused (it is Stage 7 of the rework plan, never built). `KeyS` also added to `TRANSLATING_KEYS` so a wedged backpedal is visible to `detectHeldKeyNoMovement`. **Caveat, stated plainly: unit-tested but never observed firing in play.** Across ~118,000 logged decisions in four runs (8 levels x 4, 3 levels x 3, 3 levels x 8), `mineRetreat` fired **zero** times, so `KeyS` never appeared. The original capture had 40 `mineRetreat` rows, so the branch does fire sometimes — the encounter is simply much rarer than that one capture suggested. Two earlier "it's fixed" readings in this session were invalid for exactly that reason: the post-fix runs had zero `mineRetreat` rows, so 0 oscillation findings measured the encounter not happening, not the fix working. **Do not A/B this until it has been seen firing** — the event is likely too rare to move any aggregate metric



